window.QUIZZES_HAND={"s2scicd":[{"q":"What does workload identity federation remove from a CI pipeline?","options":["The need for a cloud account","A long-lived cloud access key stored in repository secrets","The need to run tests","The deployment step"],"answer":1,"why":"The pipeline proves who it is with a short-lived signed token instead of holding a stored credential, so there is nothing to leak or rotate.","whyWrong":["You still need one.","","Unrelated to CI stages.","Deployment still happens; only the credential changes."]},{"q":"What proves which pipeline is running?","options":["The source IP address","An OIDC token signed by the CI platform, whose claims describe the job","The repository name in the request body","A shared secret in an environment variable"],"answer":1,"why":"The CI platform mints a token per job; its iss, aud and sub claims describe exactly which repository, workflow and ref is running.","whyWrong":["CI runners have no stable addresses.","","Anything in a request body is attacker-controlled.","That is the stored secret this replaces."]},{"q":"Your cloud trust policy checks the issuer and the signature, and nothing else. What is the consequence?","options":["Nothing (the signature is proof enough)","Tokens expire too quickly","Any repository on that CI platform, including an attacker's, can assume your role","The audience check happens automatically"],"answer":2,"why":"The CI platform signs tokens for every repository it hosts. A valid signature proves origin, not entitlement, the subject condition is the actual boundary.","whyWrong":["The signature says where it came from, not who should be trusted.","Lifetime is unrelated.","","Nothing is automatic."]},{"q":"A trust policy names the repository but not the ref. What becomes possible?","options":["Nothing; the repository is the security boundary","The token cannot be verified","Only the default branch can deploy","A pull request, including one from a fork, can obtain production credentials"],"answer":3,"why":"Workflows run for pull requests too, and their tokens carry the same repository. Without a ref condition, any of them satisfies the policy.","whyWrong":["Branches within one repository are not equally trusted.","Verification is unaffected.","The opposite: any ref qualifies.",""]},{"q":"Why should the subject condition be an exact match rather than a prefix?","options":["Prefixes break the signature","A prefix on repo:acme/app also matches repo:acme/app-evil","Prefix matching is slower","Exact matching is required by OIDC"],"answer":1,"why":"Subject strings are structured, and a prefix test crosses the boundary between segments, which is precisely how a lookalike repository qualifies.","whyWrong":["The signature is over the token, not the policy.","","Performance is irrelevant here.","OIDC has no opinion on cloud trust policies."]}],"eibroker":[{"q":"What is an identity broker?","options":["A directory replication tool that keeps user records in step between two separate directories","A hub that is a relying party to upstream IdPs and an identity provider to downstream apps","A service that buys verified identity data and resells it to businesses that need to check it","A password vault that holds end-user credentials on behalf of the applications behind it"],"answer":1,"why":"Each side integrates once with the broker instead of every app integrating with every IdP.","whyWrong":["That is directory sync.","","Nothing is sold.","That is a credential manager."]},{"q":"What is the integration arithmetic a broker improves?","options":["From n x m to n + m","It removes integrations entirely","From n + m to n x m","It does not change the count"],"answer":0,"why":"Without a broker every app connects to every IdP. With one, each connects only to the hub.","whyWrong":["","Each side still integrates once.","That is the wrong direction.","It changes it substantially."]},{"q":"Two upstream providers each have a user whose sub is 12345. What happens if the broker keys accounts on the raw sub?","options":["Two unrelated people are merged into one account","Nothing (subs are globally unique)","The second login is rejected","The broker generates a new sub"],"answer":0,"why":"A subject identifier is only unique within its issuer, so the pair (issuer, sub) is the identity, never the value alone.","whyWrong":["","They are unique per issuer only.","Nothing detects the collision.","Not unless you write that code."]},{"q":"A customer running their own IdP asserts the email ceo@yourcompany.com. Why is that dangerous?","options":["Email claims are always ignored","If the broker links accounts by email, they take over that account","It is not, email claims are verified by the protocol","It breaks the signature"],"answer":1,"why":"An upstream can assert any value it likes. A claim is only trustworthy where the provider has proven authority over it, which is why domain ownership must be verified.","whyWrong":["Many implementations use them, which is the problem.","","No protocol verifies that the asserter owns the address.","The assertion is validly signed by them."]},{"q":"Which cost of brokering is structural rather than incidental?","options":["It only works with SAML, so an organization adopting OIDC has to run a second broker alongside it","It prevents multi-factor authentication, because the broker cannot forward a second factor challenge","Everything behind it stops working when it is down, and compromising it exposes every app and upstream at once","It requires substantially more storage, since every assertion has to be retained for the audit trail"],"answer":2,"why":"The broker inherits the availability requirements of every application behind it and concentrates the blast radius, the IdP-as-blast-radius argument, one level up.","whyWrong":["It is protocol-agnostic by design.","Consistent MFA policy is a reason to use one.","","Storage is trivial."]}],"sml6":[{"q":"Why is a single cutover from SAML to OIDC a bad plan?","options":["SAML cannot be disabled","Every undocumented integration is discovered at once, usually out of hours","The protocols cannot coexist","OIDC requires new hardware"],"answer":1,"why":"Run both, migrate per application, and remove the old integration as a separate later change so a rollback is a configuration flip.","whyWrong":["It can, once nothing uses it.","","They coexist perfectly well; that is the strategy.","No hardware is involved."]},{"q":"What identifier problem appears during migration?","options":["OIDC has no identifier","The SAML NameID and the OIDC sub are usually different values for the same person","Both protocols use email as the identifier","Identifiers are assigned by the application"],"answer":1,"why":"If the application stored the NameID as its user key, the OIDC login arrives looking like an entirely new user.","whyWrong":["It has sub.","","Neither should.","They come from the IdP."]},{"q":"Why must accounts NOT be auto-linked by email during a migration?","options":["Email lookups are slow at scale, so matching on a subject identifier keeps the migration within its window","Email is mutable and asserted by whichever side is speaking, so anyone able to influence it can attach to an existing account","Email claims are encrypted in most assertions, so the migration tooling cannot read them reliably","SAML does not carry email as a standard attribute, so the value would be missing for many users"],"answer":1,"why":"Explicit links populated deliberately are the safe answer; an unknown identifier means no account rather than a guess.","whyWrong":["Speed is irrelevant.","","They are not.","It usually does."]},{"q":"Which design choice makes a migration take weeks rather than a year?","options":["Applications keying on an internal account id rather than on the protocol's identifier","Migrating on a weekend","Using SAML for everything first","Choosing the same IdP vendor"],"answer":0,"why":"An internal key means the protocol identifier is a lookup input rather than the primary key, so changing protocols does not change every stored reference.","whyWrong":["","Flag days make it worse.","That is the starting state.","Vendor choice does not address stored identifiers."]},{"q":"How do you know an application's migration is complete?","options":["When the vendor confirms it","When logins by protocol, measured before you started, reach zero for SAML","When the SAML integration is deleted","When the OIDC integration is configured"],"answer":1,"why":"Instrument the login path by protocol first, then remove the old integration after a quiet period, the traffic you cannot see is what pages you.","whyWrong":["They cannot see your traffic.","","Deleting first is how you find the nightly job.","Configuration is the start, not the end."]}],"pki7":[{"q":"What does certificate pinning change about trust?","options":["It removes the need for TLS, since the pin itself authenticates the peer before any handshake","Instead of accepting any certificate from any trusted CA, the client requires a specific pre-configured key","It extends certificate lifetime, since a pinned certificate no longer depends on the CA expiry","It encrypts the connection a second time, layering a private key over the negotiated session"],"answer":1,"why":"Any of the CAs in a trust store can issue for your domain; pinning narrows acceptance to a key you chose.","whyWrong":["It is a TLS control.","","Lifetimes are unchanged.","No extra encryption is involved."]},{"q":"Modern pinning pins what, exactly?","options":["The CA's name","The certificate file","A hash of the public key (SubjectPublicKeyInfo)","The domain name"],"answer":2,"why":"Certificates are reissued routinely; if the key is carried across the renewal, a key pin survives it while a certificate pin does not.","whyWrong":["Names are not cryptographic material.","That breaks on every renewal.","","That is what the certificate already asserts."]},{"q":"Why is pinning an intermediate usually preferred to pinning the leaf?","options":["It is required by TLS 1.3","Intermediates are more secure","It survives ordinary leaf rotation while still excluding every other CA","Leaves cannot be hashed"],"answer":2,"why":"Leaf pinning makes every certificate renewal a coordinated client release; intermediate pinning keeps the narrowing without that coupling.","whyWrong":["TLS has no such requirement.","Neither is inherently stronger.","","They can."]},{"q":"You pin a single leaf key in a mobile app and then rotate the key. What happens?","options":["Clients fall back to normal CA validation","The app updates itself automatically","Installed clients refuse to connect, and no server-side change can fix it","Only new installs are affected"],"answer":2,"why":"A failed pin does not degrade gracefully, and you cannot push an update through a connection the client will not make. This is why backup pins exist.","whyWrong":["Falling back would defeat the control.","It cannot reach you to update.","","Existing installs are exactly what breaks."]},{"q":"Why was HTTP Public Key Pinning (HPKP) removed from browsers?","options":["It was never implemented","It was replaced by a better header","Browsers stopped supporting TLS","Sites bricked themselves, and a hostile pin could deny service to a domain"],"answer":3,"why":"The failure mode was severe and irreversible for the site's own users, which is why pinning survives in applications you control rather than in browsers.","whyWrong":["It shipped and was later removed.","Nothing equivalent replaced it.","Browsers still use TLS.",""]}],"s2sx":[{"q":"What problem does token exchange exist to solve?","options":["Encrypting tokens in transit, so an intermediary in the call chain cannot read the claims inside","Letting users authenticate without a password, by exchanging a device signal for a session token","A service needs to call another service on a user's behalf, but the token it holds was minted for itself","Making tokens smaller, by swapping a large assertion for a compact reference the API can resolve"],"answer":2,"why":"The token you were handed is addressed to you. The next service needs one addressed to it, still tied to the same user.","whyWrong":["Transport security is TLS's job.","That is authentication, a different protocol.","","Size is not the concern."]},{"q":"In an exchange request, which parameter says WHO the work is being done for?","options":["actor_token","subject_token","audience","scope"],"answer":1,"why":"The subject token identifies the principal the new token will be about, usually the user whose request started everything.","whyWrong":["The actor token says who is ASKING.","","audience says where the new token may be used.","scope says what it may do."]},{"q":"Why is forwarding the user's original token to every downstream service a bad idea?","options":["Tokens cannot be presented twice, so the second service in the chain would be rejected outright","The user would have to log in again, because forwarding consumes the token's single permitted use","Every service then holds a credential that works at every other service, so one compromise spreads sideways","It is slower, since every downstream hop has to validate a token it did not request itself"],"answer":2,"why":"An over-broad token turns a single compromised workload into access to everything the user could reach, and it defeats audience validation.","whyWrong":["Nothing prevents re-sending a token.","The user is not involved.","","Forwarding is the fast option; that is its appeal."]},{"q":"A token issued by delegation differs from one issued by impersonation in what way?","options":["Delegation records the acting service in an act claim; impersonation records only the user","Delegation requires PKCE","Delegation tokens last longer","Impersonation tokens are encrypted"],"answer":0,"why":"Delegation keeps both parties visible, so downstream services and audit logs know the user AND which service acted. Impersonation is indistinguishable from the user acting directly.","whyWrong":["","PKCE belongs to the authorization code flow.","Lifetime is unrelated to the choice.","Neither is encrypted by default."]},{"q":"Service A exchanges a user token and calls B; B exchanges again and calls C. In the token C receives, what should the claims look like?","options":["sub is service B, and the user is lost","sub is still the user, with act naming B and the nested act naming A","sub is A and act is B","Each hop replaces act, so only B appears"],"answer":1,"why":"The subject stays the original user at every hop, and each new actor nests the previous one, so the chain reads most-recent-first and the whole path stays attributable.","whyWrong":["Losing the user defeats the purpose of the exchange.","","The subject is never a service in this flow.","Replacing act erases the middle of the chain, a common implementation bug."]}],"s2stxn":[{"q":"What is a Transaction Token?","options":["A replacement for TLS between services, carrying the confidentiality the transport would provide","A short-lived signed JWT carrying the identity and context of one request through an internal call chain","A long-lived credential for machine-to-machine calls","An encrypted refresh token, issued so that intermediaries in the call chain cannot read it"],"answer":1,"why":"It is minted at the boundary for one transaction and passed along every internal hop that transaction causes.","whyWrong":["It complements transport security rather than replacing it.","","They live for minutes and are bound to one transaction.","No encryption is implied, and refresh tokens are explicitly excluded."]},{"q":"In a Txn-Token, what does the aud claim identify?","options":["The trust domain in which it is valid","The user it describes","The workload that requested it","The single service that may accept it"],"answer":0,"why":"Deliberately unlike an access token: the audience is a whole trust domain, so many workloads inside the boundary may accept it and nothing outside may.","whyWrong":["","That is sub.","That is req_wl.","That is how an access token's audience works, and this inverts it."]},{"q":"What is the relationship between RFC 8693 and Transaction Tokens?","options":["Txn-Tokens replaced RFC 8693","They are unrelated","RFC 8693 token exchange is the mechanism used to request a Txn-Token from the TTS","They are the same specification"],"answer":2,"why":"A Txn-Token is obtained with an ordinary token-exchange request whose requested_token_type names the txn_token type. Exchange is the how; the transaction token is the what.","whyWrong":["RFC 8693 is unchanged and widely used for other purposes.","One is built directly on the other.","","RFC 8693 is a finished RFC; transaction tokens are a separate draft."]},{"q":"A compromised service in the middle of a call chain changes the transfer amount before calling the next service. Which part of the design catches it?","options":["The exp claim","The signed tctx claim carrying the immutable parameters","Mutual TLS between the services","The txn claim"],"answer":1,"why":"tctx holds the parameters that must not change, and it is covered by the TTS's signature, so a downstream workload can detect that what it was asked to do differs from what the customer requested.","whyWrong":["exp bounds lifetime, not content.","","mTLS authenticates the channel, and a compromised service has a legitimate one.","txn correlates the transaction; it does not carry parameters."]},{"q":"Which statement about Txn-Tokens is accurate?","options":["They are valid across trust domains once signed","They prevent replay entirely within the trust domain","They do not prevent replay inside the trust domain during their lifetime, which is why lifetimes are minutes","They can be refreshed like access tokens"],"answer":2,"why":"The draft is explicit that replay within the domain and lifetime remains possible, so short expiry and narrow scope are the mitigations rather than an afterthought.","whyWrong":["The aud claim confines them to one trust domain.","No token achieves that on its own.","","A refresh token may not even be used as the subject of the request."]}],"idf0":[{"q":"What does IdP stand for?","options":["Integrated Directory","Internal Data Protocol","Identity Policy","Identity Provider"],"answer":3,"why":"The IdP is the authority that authenticates users and issues tokens or assertions.","whyWrong":["A directory stores users; the IdP authenticates them.","That is not what IdP means.","A policy is a rule set, not the authenticating authority.",""]},{"q":"What does OIDC stand for?","options":["Online ID Check","OpenID Connect","OAuth Identity Config","Open Identity Credential"],"answer":1,"why":"OpenID Connect is the authentication layer built on top of OAuth 2.0.","whyWrong":["Not a real term.","","Not a real term.","Not a real term."]},{"q":"What does MFA mean?","options":["Multi-Factor Authentication","Master File Access","Managed Federation Access","Multi-Field Authorization"],"answer":0,"why":"MFA requires two or more independent factors to sign in.","whyWrong":["","Made up.","Made up.","Made up."]},{"q":"What does SSO mean?","options":["Secure Socket Option","Session Storage Object","Signed Security Officer","Single Sign-On"],"answer":3,"why":"Single Sign-On lets one login reach many applications.","whyWrong":["Not a real term.","Not a real term.","Not a real term.",""]}],"idffed":[{"q":"In federation, which party authenticates the user and issues the signed proof?","options":["Relying Party (RP)","The browser","Identity Provider (IdP)","The API gateway"],"answer":2,"why":"The IdP vouches for the user; everyone else relies on that.","whyWrong":["The RP relies on the proof, it does not create it.","The browser only carries the proof.","","A gateway routes traffic; it does not authenticate the user."]},{"q":"What does the Service Provider / Relying Party do?","options":["Stores every user password","Signs the token","Relies on the IdP proof instead of checking a password","Runs the MFA prompt"],"answer":2,"why":"The whole point of federation is that the app trusts the IdP rather than holding passwords.","whyWrong":["That is what federation avoids.","The IdP signs the token, not the RP.","","The IdP handles authentication and MFA."]},{"q":"Why can a random website not fake \"Google says this is you\"?","options":["It cannot be prevented","The RP only accepts tokens signed by the pre-trusted IdP key","Google emails a confirmation","Browsers block it"],"answer":1,"why":"Trust is set up in advance with the IdP public keys, so only real signatures verify.","whyWrong":["It absolutely can be prevented by signature verification.","","No email is involved in token verification.","The browser does not verify the signature; the RP does."]}],"idffed2":[{"q":"Which piece is the authority that authenticates users?","options":["Relying Party","Identity Provider (IdP)","Access token","JWKS endpoint"],"answer":1,"why":"The IdP is the authenticating authority.","whyWrong":["The relying party consumes the proof.","","A token is the proof, not the authority.","JWKS just publishes the keys."]},{"q":"What is the \"proof\" passed in federation?","options":["A session cookie only","A signed token or assertion","A TLS certificate","A username and password"],"answer":1,"why":"The IdP issues a signed token (OIDC) or assertion (SAML) the app can verify.","whyWrong":["A cookie tracks a local session, it is not the federated proof.","","A certificate proves a server, not the user login.","Passwords are what federation avoids sending around."]},{"q":"Where does the IdP publish the keys used to verify its tokens?","options":["In the client secret","A JWKS endpoint / SAML metadata","Inside the access token","In the user cookie"],"answer":1,"why":"The IdP publishes public keys at JWKS (OIDC) or in metadata (SAML) so relying parties can verify signatures.","whyWrong":["The client secret authenticates the app, it is not the IdP signing key.","","The token carries a signature, not the verification key.","Cookies do not carry IdP keys."]}],"am2":[{"q":"A password is which kind of authentication factor?","options":["Something you have (possession)","Something you know (knowledge)","Something you are (inherence)","Somewhere you are"],"answer":1,"why":"A password is knowledge, a secret you remember.","whyWrong":["Possession is a device or key you hold.","","Inherence is a biometric.","Location is a context signal, not this factor."]},{"q":"A one-time code from an authenticator app is which factor?","options":["Something you have (possession)","Something you are","A recovery factor","Something you know"],"answer":0,"why":"The code comes from a device you possess.","whyWrong":["","Inherence is a biometric.","Recovery is a flow, not a factor category.","Knowledge is a memorized secret."]},{"q":"A fingerprint is which factor?","options":["Something you know","Something you are (inherence)","Something you have","A knowledge factor"],"answer":1,"why":"A biometric is inherence, something you are.","whyWrong":["Knowledge is memorized.","","Possession is a held device.","A knowledge factor is a memorised secret, not a biometric."]},{"q":"True MFA combines factors from…","options":["the same category twice","one strong factor","two or more different categories","two passwords"],"answer":2,"why":"The categories must differ, so stealing one (a password) is not enough.","whyWrong":["Same category twice is not multi-factor.","One factor, however strong, is single-factor.","","Two passwords are both knowledge, still one factor type."]}],"am6":[{"q":"Which NIST scale measures how strongly the real-world identity was proofed at enrollment?","options":["FAL (Federation Assurance Level)","TLS level","IAL (Identity Assurance Level)","AAL (Authenticator Assurance Level)"],"answer":2,"why":"IAL is about identity proofing.","whyWrong":["FAL is about how the assertion is protected.","TLS is transport security, not an identity scale.","","AAL is about login strength, not proofing."]},{"q":"Which scale measures how strong the login authentication is?","options":["MFA-2","IAL","FAL","AAL"],"answer":3,"why":"AAL rates authenticator strength (single factor, MFA, hardware).","whyWrong":["Not a real scale.","IAL rates identity proofing.","FAL rates the federated assertion.",""]},{"q":"MFA corresponds to which authenticator level?","options":["AAL2","AAL1","AAL3","IAL2"],"answer":0,"why":"AAL2 requires two factors (MFA).","whyWrong":["","AAL1 is a single factor.","AAL3 additionally requires a hardware, phishing-resistant authenticator.","IAL2 is a proofing level, not an authenticator level."]},{"q":"A hardware, phishing-resistant authenticator (security key) is…","options":["AAL3","FAL1","IAL1","AAL1"],"answer":0,"why":"AAL3 is the highest authenticator level and requires hardware-backed, phishing-resistant auth.","whyWrong":["","FAL is about assertions, not authenticators.","IAL1 is self-asserted identity, unrelated.","AAL1 is single factor."]}],"am8":[{"q":"Creating a new passkey key pair for a site is which WebAuthn ceremony?","options":["Authentication (assertion)","Registration (attestation)","Recovery","Rotation"],"answer":1,"why":"Registration creates the key pair and may attest the authenticator.","whyWrong":["Authentication signs a challenge with an existing key.","","Recovery is a separate account flow.","Rotation is not a WebAuthn ceremony."]},{"q":"Signing a server challenge to log in is which ceremony?","options":["Authentication (assertion)","Enrollment","Provisioning","Registration (attestation)"],"answer":0,"why":"Authentication has the authenticator sign a fresh challenge (an assertion).","whyWrong":["","Enrollment is another word for registration, not login.","Provisioning creates accounts, not this signature.","Registration is the one-time key-pair creation."]},{"q":"In WebAuthn, the private key…","options":["is the client secret","is stored on the server","never leaves the device","is emailed to the user"],"answer":2,"why":"The private key stays in the device, which is what makes passkeys phishing-resistant.","whyWrong":["That is an OAuth concept, not a passkey.","The server only stores the public key.","","Keys are never emailed."]}],"az2":[{"q":"In RBAC, permissions are granted to a user through…","options":["roles","their IP address","the session id","the request path"],"answer":0,"why":"Users get roles; roles carry permissions.","whyWrong":["","IP is a network detail, not an RBAC grant.","A session id identifies a login, not permissions.","The path is what is protected, not how access is granted."]},{"q":"A \"viewer\" role typically has which permission?","options":["admin","read, write, delete","write","read"],"answer":3,"why":"A viewer can read only, least privilege.","whyWrong":["Admin is the most powerful role, not a viewer.","That is an admin-level set.","Viewers do not write.",""]},{"q":"Which principle says a role should hold only the access it truly needs?","options":["Fail-closed","Separation of duties","Defense in depth","Least privilege"],"answer":3,"why":"Least privilege grants the minimum access required.","whyWrong":["Fail-closed is a default-deny behavior, not this principle.","Separation of duties splits conflicting powers between people.","Defense in depth is layered controls.",""]}],"oaclient":[{"q":"A single-page app (SPA) is which kind of OAuth client?","options":["A resource server","An identity provider","Public (uses PKCE, no secret)","Confidential, uses a client secret"],"answer":2,"why":"A SPA cannot hide a secret, so it is a public client and uses PKCE.","whyWrong":["The resource server is the API, not the app.","The IdP is the authority, not the app.","","It cannot keep a secret, so it is not confidential."]},{"q":"How does a server-side (confidential) client authenticate to the token endpoint?","options":["With a client secret or a key","With a session cookie","With the user password","It cannot authenticate"],"answer":0,"why":"Confidential clients prove themselves with a secret or an asymmetric key.","whyWrong":["","Cookies are for browser sessions, not client auth.","The user password is never used by the client this way.","Confidential clients absolutely authenticate."]},{"q":"Which client authentication method uses no shared secret?","options":["private_key_jwt (asymmetric key)","client_secret_basic","client_secret_post","HTTP Basic with the password"],"answer":0,"why":"private_key_jwt signs with a private key; the server verifies with the public key, nothing shared to leak.","whyWrong":["","Uses a shared secret.","Uses a shared secret.","Uses a password, the weakest option."]},{"q":"Where should a client_secret be stored?","options":["A secret manager or server environment, never in front-end code","In the redirect URL","In localStorage","In the SPA bundle"],"answer":0,"why":"Anything shipped to the browser is public, so secrets stay server-side.","whyWrong":["","URLs get logged and shared.","localStorage is readable by any script (XSS).","The bundle is downloadable by anyone."]}],"oa10":[{"q":"Best flow for a web or mobile app acting for a user?","options":["Client Credentials","Implicit","ROPC (password)","Authorization Code + PKCE"],"answer":3,"why":"Authorization Code with PKCE is the modern default for user-facing apps.","whyWrong":["Client Credentials is for machines with no user.","Implicit is deprecated.","ROPC (password grant) is deprecated.",""]},{"q":"Best flow for machine-to-machine (no user present)?","options":["Client Credentials","Device flow","Implicit","Authorization Code"],"answer":0,"why":"Client Credentials authenticates the service itself, with no user.","whyWrong":["","Device flow is for input-limited devices with a user.","Implicit is deprecated and user-facing.","Authorization Code needs a user at a browser."]},{"q":"Which grant is deprecated and should be avoided?","options":["Refresh","Authorization Code + PKCE","Client Credentials","Implicit / ROPC"],"answer":3,"why":"Implicit and ROPC are discouraged by the OAuth Security BCP.","whyWrong":["Refresh tokens are still used (with rotation).","This is the recommended flow.","Still valid for M2M.",""]}],"s2s6":[{"q":"Inside a Kubernetes cluster or service mesh, how do services usually prove identity to each other?","options":["mTLS with workload certificates (e.g. SPIFFE/SPIRE)","IP allowlists only","No auth inside the cluster","A shared password in env vars"],"answer":0,"why":"Each workload gets a short-lived certificate and services verify each other with mutual TLS.","whyWrong":["","IP can be spoofed and is not identity.","Zero trust means never assuming the network is safe.","Shared static secrets are exactly what this avoids."]},{"q":"Machine-to-machine access to an external API is typically obtained via…","options":["a hardcoded API key in the repo","SAML browser SSO","the user session cookie","OAuth client-credentials tokens"],"answer":3,"why":"A service gets its own token from a minting authority with the client-credentials grant.","whyWrong":["Hardcoded keys leak and cannot rotate cleanly.","SAML browser SSO needs a human at a browser.","There is no user session in M2M.",""]},{"q":"Zero trust means…","options":["authenticate once at the perimeter only","verify identity and authorize every request, regardless of network location","trust anything inside the firewall","trust by IP address"],"answer":1,"why":"Never trust by location; authenticate and authorize each call.","whyWrong":["Perimeter-only auth is what zero trust rejects.","","That is the old perimeter model zero trust replaces.","IP is not identity."]}],"ei2":[{"q":"In Kerberos, what does the Authentication Service (AS) issue?","options":["A refresh token","A service ticket","A Ticket-Granting Ticket (TGT)","A password"],"answer":2,"why":"The AS verifies you once and issues a TGT.","whyWrong":["Refresh tokens are an OAuth concept.","The TGS issues service tickets, using your TGT.","","Passwords are never handed out."]},{"q":"To reach a specific service you present the TGT to the TGS and receive…","options":["a SAML assertion","another TGT","your password back","a service ticket"],"answer":3,"why":"The TGS grants a per-service ticket so your password never reaches the service.","whyWrong":["SAML is a different protocol.","You already have the TGT; the TGS gives a service ticket.","Passwords are not returned.",""]}],"ei3":[{"q":"A new hire (Joiner) should trigger which provisioning action?","options":["Deactivate the account","Nothing","Create the account","Update access"],"answer":2,"why":"Joiner creates the account and grants baseline access.","whyWrong":["Deactivate is the Leaver case.","Onboarding must create access.","","Update is the Mover case."]},{"q":"An employee who leaves (Leaver) should trigger…","options":["Nothing automatic","Deactivate/delete the account","Create a new account","Only a password reset"],"answer":1,"why":"Leaver deprovisions access, the biggest security win of automation.","whyWrong":["Leaving accounts active is a top breach cause.","","That is the Joiner case.","A reset does not remove access."]},{"q":"Which standard automates pushing these create/update/deactivate operations?","options":["SCIM","LDAP browsing","SAML","OAuth"],"answer":0,"why":"SCIM is the provisioning API that syncs accounts across systems.","whyWrong":["","LDAP is a directory query protocol, not the provisioning push.","SAML carries login assertions, not provisioning.","OAuth is authorization for API access."]}],"ei4b":[{"q":"Workforce with an HR system of record is best served by…","options":["JIT only","Manual creation","SCIM pre-provisioning","No provisioning"],"answer":2,"why":"SCIM keeps accounts in sync from the HR source and supports clean deprovisioning.","whyWrong":["JIT alone will not deprovision leavers.","Manual does not scale for a workforce.","","Some provisioning is required."]},{"q":"A huge, unpredictable external / customer population is best served by…","options":["Manual creation","LDAP sync","JIT provisioning","Pre-importing every user"],"answer":2,"why":"JIT creates the account on first login, so you never pre-import millions of users.","whyWrong":["Manual cannot scale to consumers.","LDAP sync fits a directory-backed workforce, not open consumers.","","Pre-importing millions is impractical."]},{"q":"The classic gap with JIT provisioning is that it…","options":["creates accounts but does not deprovision them","cannot create accounts","requires SAML","is slower than manual"],"answer":0,"why":"JIT provisions on first login but never removes access, so pair it with SCIM or reviews.","whyWrong":["","Creating accounts is exactly what JIT does.","JIT works with OIDC or SAML.","JIT is far faster than manual."]}],"biz":[{"q":"Netflix serving individual consumers is an example of…","options":["P2P","B2C","B2B","B2B2C"],"answer":1,"why":"Selling directly to individual consumers is B2C.","whyWrong":["P2P is not a business model here.","","B2B serves other businesses.","B2B2C serves a business that serves its own consumers."]},{"q":"Salesforce serving other companies (each a tenant) is…","options":["B2C","B2B","B2B2C","C2C"],"answer":1,"why":"Selling to businesses, whose employees are the users, is B2B.","whyWrong":["B2C is direct-to-consumer.","","B2B2C adds the tenant serving its own consumers.","C2C is consumer-to-consumer."]},{"q":"Shopify (merchants who serve their own shoppers through the platform) is…","options":["B2B","B2B2C","B2G","B2C"],"answer":1,"why":"You serve a business and, through it, that business own consumers, two identity layers.","whyWrong":["B2B stops at the business tenant.","","B2G is business-to-government.","B2C is direct to consumers only."]},{"q":"Which models are inherently multi-tenant?","options":["none","B2C only","B2C and B2B","B2B and B2B2C"],"answer":3,"why":"Serving businesses means each customer is an isolated tenant.","whyWrong":["Multi-tenancy is central to B2B and B2B2C.","B2C is single-population, not tenant-based.","B2C is not multi-tenant.",""]}],"ao3":[{"q":"PAR (Pushed Authorization Requests) improves security by…","options":["rotating refresh tokens","sending the request parameters to the server first, off the browser URL","hashing passwords","encrypting the database"],"answer":1,"why":"PAR moves sensitive parameters off the front channel into a back-channel push.","whyWrong":["That is refresh-token rotation.","","That is password storage.","Unrelated to database encryption."]},{"q":"RAR (Rich Authorization Requests) provides…","options":["fine-grained, structured authorization details beyond coarse scopes","a faster token endpoint","a new password hash","mutual TLS"],"answer":0,"why":"RAR replaces blunt scopes with structured detail like \"transfer up to 500 from account X\".","whyWrong":["","It is about detail, not speed.","Nothing to do with passwords.","mTLS is a client-auth mechanism, not RAR."]},{"q":"DPoP is about…","options":["compressing tokens","user consent screens","password storage","sender-constrained (proof-of-possession) tokens"],"answer":3,"why":"DPoP binds a token to a key the client must prove, so a stolen token is useless.","whyWrong":["Not compression.","Not consent.","Not password storage.",""]}],"ao5":[{"q":"Which defense stops CSRF on the OAuth redirect?","options":["The state parameter","A nonce","PKCE","Exact redirect_uri matching"],"answer":0,"why":"The state parameter is a random value the client checks on return, blocking CSRF.","whyWrong":["","nonce defends ID-token replay, not the redirect CSRF.","PKCE defends code interception.","Exact redirect_uri defends open redirect / mix-up."]},{"q":"Which defends against authorization-code interception by public clients?","options":["CORS","state","PKCE","nonce"],"answer":2,"why":"PKCE ties the code to a secret only the real client holds, so a stolen code is useless.","whyWrong":["CORS is a browser policy, not this defense.","state defends CSRF.","","nonce defends replay of ID tokens."]},{"q":"Which defends against replay of a token or ID token?","options":["state","exact redirect_uri","A one-time nonce","a longer secret"],"answer":2,"why":"A nonce (plus short expiry) makes a captured token single-use.","whyWrong":["state defends CSRF on the redirect.","redirect_uri matching defends open redirect.","","Length alone does not stop replay."]},{"q":"Open redirect / mix-up is mitigated by…","options":["disabling PKCE","wildcards in redirect_uri","longer tokens","registering and matching exact redirect_uri values"],"answer":3,"why":"Only exact, pre-registered redirect URIs should be accepted.","whyWrong":["Disabling PKCE weakens security.","Wildcards are the vulnerability, not the fix.","Token length is unrelated.",""]}],"ig5":[{"q":"CIAM (Customer IAM) primarily serves…","options":["employees","service accounts","customers / consumers","admins only"],"answer":2,"why":"CIAM is about external consumers, optimizing self-service and scale.","whyWrong":["Employees are workforce IAM.","Service accounts are machine identity.","","Admins are a small subset, not the focus."]},{"q":"Workforce IAM primarily serves…","options":["external customers","the public","employees and contractors","IoT devices"],"answer":2,"why":"Workforce IAM governs staff, emphasizing control and deprovisioning.","whyWrong":["Customers are CIAM.","The public is CIAM territory.","","Devices are workload/machine identity."]}],"idcap":[{"q":"In the auth lifecycle, the \"register\" step should…","options":["revoke the session","hash the password (never store plaintext)","validate a token","issue tokens"],"answer":1,"why":"Registration stores a slow, salted hash of the password.","whyWrong":["Revoking is logout.","","Validation happens on each request.","Issuing tokens happens at login."]},{"q":"On \"login\", the server…","options":["issues tokens (access + refresh)","deletes the account","rotates signing keys","hashes the password"],"answer":0,"why":"After verifying credentials, login mints the tokens.","whyWrong":["","Login does not delete anything.","Key rotation is an operational task, not login.","Hashing is a registration step."]},{"q":"On each \"request\", the server…","options":["creates the account","re-hashes the password","issues a new refresh token","validates the access token, then authorizes the action"],"answer":3,"why":"Every request checks the token (iss/aud/exp) and then the permission.","whyWrong":["The account already exists.","No password is involved per request.","Refresh happens on the refresh endpoint, not every request.",""]},{"q":"On \"logout\", the server…","options":["hashes a password","creates a session","revokes the session","issues a token"],"answer":2,"why":"Logout must end the session server-side, not just drop the cookie.","whyWrong":["Hashing is registration.","Creating a session is login.","","Tokens are issued at login."]}],"am9":[{"q":"Where do most account breaches actually come from?","options":["Credential stuffing (replaying passwords leaked elsewhere)","Brute-forcing strong passwords","Zero-day exploits","Tricking admins in person"],"answer":0,"why":"Attackers replay username/password pairs leaked from other sites, betting on reuse, cheap and automated.","whyWrong":["","Brute-forcing strong passwords is slow and usually rate-limited.","Zero-days are rare and expensive next to stuffing.","That happens but is not the bulk of takeovers."]},{"q":"Which single control makes a leaked password insufficient on its own?","options":["A longer password policy","Security questions","Login email notifications","MFA"],"answer":3,"why":"With MFA, a correct-but-leaked password still cannot complete the login.","whyWrong":["Length does not help once the exact password has leaked.","Knowledge answers are also leakable and guessable.","Notifications warn after the fact; they do not block.",""]},{"q":"Checking a new password against known-breached lists is done safely with…","options":["downloading every breach in full","an email round-trip","sending the full password to a third party","k-anonymity (only a hash prefix leaves you)"],"answer":3,"why":"Services like Have I Been Pwned use k-anonymity, so the password itself never leaves your server.","whyWrong":["Impractical and unnecessary.","Unrelated to breach checks.","You must never send the full password.",""]},{"q":"A flood of failed logins across many accounts is best stopped by…","options":["a CAPTCHA on every page","taking the site offline","longer sessions","rate limiting + bot detection"],"answer":3,"why":"Throttle the automation and detect bots; keep humans unblocked.","whyWrong":["CAPTCHA everywhere punishes real users, a last resort.","That is denial of service to yourself.","Unrelated to the attack.",""]}],"iddid":[{"q":"In decentralized identity, where do your credentials live?","options":["In your own wallet","In a shared database","Only in the browser cache","On a central IdP server"],"answer":0,"why":"The holder keeps Verifiable Credentials in a wallet and presents them directly.","whyWrong":["","They are not held in a shared database.","A wallet is durable storage, not just cache.","There is no central IdP mediating each sign-in."]},{"q":"A Verifiable Credential is…","options":["a username and password","a signed, tamper-evident claim issued by an authority","a session cookie","an IP allowlist"],"answer":1,"why":"A VC is a cryptographically signed claim (e.g. \"over 18\") you can present and others can verify.","whyWrong":["It is not a password-style credential.","","A cookie is a local session token.","Unrelated to VCs."]},{"q":"Selective disclosure lets you…","options":["reset your password","share your whole profile at once","prove you are over 18 without revealing your birthdate","skip verification entirely"],"answer":2,"why":"Selective disclosure / zero-knowledge proofs reveal only the fact needed, nothing more.","whyWrong":["Unrelated.","That is the opposite of the goal.","","Verification still happens on the disclosed fact."]},{"q":"Compared with federation, decentralized identity…","options":["cannot be verified without the issuer online","is exactly the same thing","has no central IdP that sees every login","still requires a central IdP"],"answer":2,"why":"No central authority mediates each sign-in, which improves privacy and resilience.","whyWrong":["A verifier checks the signature without contacting the issuer.","It is a distinct model from federation.","","It removes the central IdP."]}],"igaudit":[{"q":"To prove who did what and when, you need…","options":["a bigger cache","more RAM","a faster CPU","an immutable audit log"],"answer":3,"why":"An append-only, tamper-evident audit trail is the evidence auditors and investigators need.","whyWrong":["Caching is unrelated.","Hardware does not make actions provable.","Speed is unrelated to auditability.",""]},{"q":"Real-time detection of suspicious login patterns is the job of…","options":["the password hasher","the stylesheet","a SIEM","the DNS resolver"],"answer":2,"why":"A SIEM ingests the logs and alerts on patterns like failed-login bursts or a new admin.","whyWrong":["Hashing protects stored passwords, not detection.","Unrelated.","","Unrelated."]},{"q":"Which must NEVER appear in identity logs?","options":["the timestamp","the action name","full tokens or secrets","the user id"],"answer":2,"why":"Logging secrets or full tokens turns your logs into a breach; log identifiers and metadata only.","whyWrong":["Timestamps are required.","The action is the whole point of the log.","","The subject id is expected and useful."]},{"q":"Frameworks like SOC 2 and NIST 800-63 map their controls to identity practices such as…","options":["screen colors","marketing copy","the database vendor","MFA, least privilege, deprovisioning, audit logs"],"answer":3,"why":"Those frameworks require exactly these identity controls, evidenced by your audit trail.","whyWrong":["Not a security control.","Not a control.","Not a control.",""]}],"idflife":[{"q":"Identity proofing happens at which point?","options":["Once, before the account exists","Never, it is automatic","Every time the user logs in","Only when the password is reset"],"answer":0,"why":"Proofing establishes who the person is in the real world, before an account is created. Authentication is the repeated step afterwards.","whyWrong":["","It is a deliberate process, not something that happens by itself.","That is authentication (proofing is not repeated per login.)","A reset re-binds a credential; it does not re-establish the real-world identity."]},{"q":"What is the difference between an authenticator and a credential?","options":["They are two words for the same thing","An authenticator is the thing you hold; a credential is the stored binding to your identifier","A credential is always a password","An authenticator is always hardware"],"answer":1,"why":"You possess an authenticator (password, passkey, security key); the system stores a credential recording that it belongs to your account.","whyWrong":["They are distinct: NIST separates them precisely because the attacks differ.","","A password is one kind of authenticator; a credential is the stored binding.","Authenticators can be knowledge or biometric, not just hardware."]},{"q":"An organisation fails an audit for accounts belonging to people who left years ago. Which lifecycle step broke?","options":["Deprovisioning","Authentication","Identity proofing","Credential binding"],"answer":0,"why":"Deprovisioning, the leaver step, is the one most often skipped, leaving orphaned accounts with live access.","whyWrong":["","Authentication is working correctly, which is exactly the problem.","Proofing was fine; these were real employees.","Binding worked; that is why the accounts still function."]},{"q":"Why should an application key its user records on a stable internal id rather than an email address?","options":["Email addresses can change or be reassigned to a different person","Emails are too long to index","Emails are encrypted","It makes no practical difference"],"answer":0,"why":"An identifier that can be reassigned will eventually attach one person’s history to another.","whyWrong":["","Length is not the issue.","Emails in a directory are not encrypted, and that would not help.","It matters a great deal, usually discovered years later."]},{"q":"One person has a work account, a Google account and a customer account at the same shop. How many people is that?","options":["Three","Three identities and no person","It depends on the protocol","One person with three accounts"],"answer":3,"why":"A person is not an account. One human routinely holds many accounts, each with its own identifier.","whyWrong":["Accounts are not people; conflating them is the mistake this lesson exists to prevent.","There is very much a person; the accounts describe them.","The distinction holds regardless of protocol.",""]}],"idfclaim":[{"q":"What turns a stored attribute into a claim?","options":["Adding it to a database index","Being asserted by a specific issuer, in transit","Encrypting it","Storing it in a directory"],"answer":1,"why":"An attribute is a fact at rest; it becomes a claim when an issuer asserts it to someone else.","whyWrong":["Indexing is a storage detail.","","Encryption changes who can read it, not what kind of fact it is.","That is what makes it an attribute in the first place."]},{"q":"Which claim should an application treat as the identity of the user?","options":["sub","preferred_username","email","name"],"answer":0,"why":"sub is immutable and scoped to the issuer, so it never gets reassigned to a different person.","whyWrong":["","A username can be released and reassigned.","Email addresses get changed and reused.","Names are not unique and change frequently."]},{"q":"What does the aud claim tell a verifier?","options":["Which algorithm signed it","Who issued the token","Who this token is intended for","When it expires"],"answer":2,"why":"aud names the intended recipient. A token minted for a different API is not valid for yours.","whyWrong":["That is alg, in the header.","That is iss.","","That is exp."]},{"q":"An IdP issues a token claiming \"role\":\"admin\", but roles are defined by your application. What should you conclude?","options":["The user is an admin; the signature is valid","The claim is only as good as the issuer authority over that fact","Any signed claim must be honoured","The token is forged"],"answer":1,"why":"A valid signature proves who said it, not that they had any business saying it. Trust an issuer only for facts it is authoritative over.","whyWrong":["A valid signature does not confer authority over your application concepts.","","Honouring every signed claim is how privilege escalation happens.","Nothing suggests forgery, the problem is authority, not authenticity."]},{"q":"Why do federation integrations often authenticate successfully but create broken user records?","options":["Weak passwords","Expired certificates","Mismatched attribute mapping between the IdP and the app","Slow networks"],"answer":2,"why":"Every system names attributes differently, and a wrong mapping still passes signature checks, the login works, the data lands in the wrong fields.","whyWrong":["Passwords are not involved in a federated login.","An expired certificate breaks the login itself, not just the record.","","Latency does not corrupt records."]}],"idftok":[{"q":"What is a token, in one sentence?","options":["A hashing algorithm","A string standing for a verified fact, issued by an authority and presented later","An encrypted password","A type of cookie"],"answer":1,"why":"You prove yourself once and receive a string you present instead of proving yourself again.","whyWrong":["Hashing is a technique used inside tokens, not the token itself.","","A token is not a password in any form; that is the point of it.","A cookie is one way to carry a token, not what a token is."]},{"q":"What makes a token \"opaque\"?","options":["It cannot expire","It carries no readable meaning and must be looked up at the issuer","It is encrypted","It is very short"],"answer":1,"why":"An opaque token is a random lookup key; the real data stays in the issuer store.","whyWrong":["Opaque tokens expire too, and can also be revoked instantly.","","Encryption is a different thing (an opaque token is usually just random.)","Length is incidental, though they do tend to be shorter."]},{"q":"Can anyone who intercepts a JWT read its claims?","options":["Only if alg is none","No, the signature encrypts the payload","Yes (base64url is encoding, not encryption)","Only with the private key"],"answer":2,"why":"The payload is merely base64url-encoded. The signature prevents tampering, not reading.","whyWrong":["The alg value has nothing to do with readability.","A signature authenticates; it does not conceal.","","No key is needed to decode; that is what JWE is for."]},{"q":"\"Access token\" and \"JWT\" describe…","options":["Two parts of the same token","The same thing","A role and a format, which vary independently","Two competing standards"],"answer":2,"why":"Access token is the job the token does; JWT is how the bytes are arranged. An access token may be a JWT or opaque.","whyWrong":["A JWT is not a component of an access token.","They answer different questions entirely.","","They are not alternatives to each other."]},{"q":"Why is revocation harder with a structured token than an opaque one?","options":["Structured tokens are longer","Structured tokens are not signed","Opaque tokens never expire","There is no central record to delete, it stays valid until it expires"],"answer":3,"why":"Self-contained tokens verify offline, so nothing central exists to invalidate. Short lifetimes plus refresh are the industry patch.","whyWrong":["Size affects bandwidth, not revocability.","They are signed; that is what makes them verifiable offline.","They do expire; they are also revocable, which is their advantage.",""]},{"q":"A verifier reads the alg field from the token header and uses whatever it says. What is the flaw?","options":["An attacker can propose alg:none and talk it out of verifying","It only works with RSA","It is slow","alg is optional"],"answer":0,"why":"The algorithm must be pinned by the verifier in advance, never chosen by the token being checked.","whyWrong":["","The flaw applies to every algorithm.","Performance is not the concern.","alg is present; the problem is obeying it."]}],"idfcast":[{"q":"SAML says \"Service Provider\". What does OAuth/OIDC call the same box?","options":["Subject","Client (or Relying Party)","Authorization Server","Resource Server"],"answer":1,"why":"The app relying on someone else authentication is the SP in SAML and the client/RP in OIDC.","whyWrong":["That is the person.","","That is the IdP counterpart, the authority.","That is the protected API, which SAML has no equivalent for."]},{"q":"Who is the \"resource owner\" in OAuth?","options":["The person who owns the data and can authorise access to it","The client application","The API holding the data","The company running the server"],"answer":0,"why":"The resource owner is the user whose data is at stake, and therefore the only one who can grant an app access to it.","whyWrong":["","The client is the app requesting access.","That is the resource server.","Ownership here is about the data subject, not the operator."]},{"q":"Which actor is the only one that mints tokens?","options":["The resource server","The browser","The authorization server","The client"],"answer":2,"why":"The authority issues tokens; clients present them and resource servers verify them.","whyWrong":["A resource server verifies and consumes them.","The browser only carries them.","","A client receives and presents tokens."]},{"q":"\"Authenticate the client\" means…","options":["Prove which application is calling","Prove which person is logged in","Check the user password","Verify the token signature"],"answer":0,"why":"A client is a registered application with its own identity, separate from the user identity.","whyWrong":["","That is authenticating the user, a different question in the same flow.","Passwords belong to users, not clients.","Signature verification is a separate step."]},{"q":"How does an application learn an OIDC authority signing keys?","options":["The user supplies them","They are derived from the client secret","They are embedded in the access token","From the discovery document jwks_uri"],"answer":3,"why":"The authority publishes /.well-known/openid-configuration, which points at the JWKS holding its public keys.","whyWrong":["Users are never involved in key distribution.","A client secret authenticates the app; it is unrelated to the issuer signing key.","A token carries a signature, not the key needed to check it.",""]}],"idf3":[{"q":"Single Sign-On is best described as…","options":["An encryption standard","A user experience: one login event, many applications","A type of token","A protocol"],"answer":1,"why":"SSO names an outcome the user perceives. It specifies no messages or actors, and can be produced several different ways.","whyWrong":["Nothing about SSO is an encryption scheme.","","SSO is not a token; tokens are one means to the end.","There is no \"SSO protocol\": SAML and OIDC are protocols that can produce it."]},{"q":"Several apps under the same domain share one session cookie, so logging into one logs you into all. Is that SSO?","options":["No, SSO requires SAML or OIDC","Only if a token is involved","No, SSO requires an IdP","Yes, and it needs no federation at all"],"answer":3,"why":"It is genuine SSO, one login, many apps, achieved with cookie scope alone. This is why \"we need SSO\" must be followed by \"across what boundary?\"","whyWrong":["Those are needed to cross an organisational boundary, not to achieve the experience.","A session cookie is enough; no token is required.","An IdP is needed for federated SSO, not for SSO as such.",""]},{"q":"An organisation federates exactly one application. What do they gain?","options":["Only faster logins","Only a nicer login page","Central MFA and policy, instant deprovisioning, and no password stored in the app","Nothing, since there is no sign-on-once benefit"],"answer":2,"why":"The structural wins are the point of federation; SSO is the side effect that appears once you federate a second app.","whyWrong":["Federation usually adds a redirect, so it is not about speed.","Appearance is incidental.","","The SSO benefit is absent, but the architectural benefits are not."]},{"q":"Why is using an OAuth access token as proof of who the user is a vulnerability?","options":["An access token authorises an app to call an API; it makes no reliable statement about the user","It is not a problem, since the token was issued only after the user authenticated successfully","Access tokens expire too quickly, so the identity they assert is stale by the time it is read","Access tokens are unsigned, so a resource server has no way to confirm who actually issued one"],"answer":0,"why":"OAuth answers \"may this app do this?\" not \"who are you?\": OpenID Connect adds the ID token precisely to answer the second question.","whyWrong":["","It is a well-documented class of vulnerability.","Expiry is unrelated to the confusion.","Many are signed; signing does not make them identity statements."]},{"q":"What distinguishes delegation from impersonation?","options":["They are the same thing, with the two names coming from different specifications describing one flow","Delegation is faster, since it avoids the extra assertion exchange that impersonation requires","Delegation records both the app and the user; impersonation makes the app indistinguishable from the user","Impersonation is always malicious, whereas delegation is the sanctioned form of the same mechanism"],"answer":2,"why":"In delegation the API can see a client acting for a user. In impersonation it sees only the user, which is more powerful and harder to audit.","whyWrong":["They are formalised as distinct modes in token exchange.","Speed is not the difference.","","Impersonation is a legitimate, deliberate pattern, it just needs care."]},{"q":"Why is Single Logout so much less reliable than Single Sign-On?","options":["The single login actually created many separate application sessions, and there is no reliable way to close them all","Logout messages are not encrypted, so intermediaries can drop them without either party noticing","Browsers block logout requests as third-party traffic, so most of them never reach the relying party","Logout was never standardized, so each vendor invented an incompatible mechanism of its own"],"answer":0,"why":"SSO is a pleasant illusion over N real sessions; ending one does not end the rest.","whyWrong":["","Encryption is not the obstacle.","Browsers do not block logout requests.","SLO is specified; it is the distributed state that makes it fragile."]}],"idfdauthn":[{"q":"In a design review, which single question tells you whether an integration is credential forwarding or federation?","options":["Whether TLS is enabled","Which protocol version is used","How long the token lasts","Where does the user type their password"],"answer":3,"why":"If the password is typed into a form your application renders, you are forwarding credentials, whatever the diagram claims.","whyWrong":["TLS protects transport; the app still holds the password.","Version numbers say nothing about credential exposure.","Lifetime is a token property, not a credential-path property.",""]},{"q":"An app collects the username and password and performs an LDAP bind to verify them. What is the main security consequence?","options":["Nothing, this is the modern default","LDAP is unencrypted","The app is inside the credential blast radius","Passwords cannot be hashed"],"answer":2,"why":"The application holds plaintext passwords in memory and logs, so compromising the app compromises every password typed into it.","whyWrong":["It is a legacy pattern, deprecated wherever a browser exists.","LDAP can run over TLS; that is a separate concern.","","The directory still hashes them; exposure is at the app."]},{"q":"Why can credential forwarding not support passkeys or SSO?","options":["Passkeys need a password first, so a forwarding proxy has nothing to present on the second step","The authority never interacts with the user directly, and no reusable login session is created","Both are proprietary, so a proxy cannot reimplement the ceremony without vendor cooperation","They require newer runtime versions than most legacy forwarding proxies are able to support"],"answer":1,"why":"Passkeys need the authenticator to talk to the authority, and SSO needs a session at the authority for a second app to reuse. Forwarding creates neither.","whyWrong":["Passkeys replace passwords rather than supplementing them.","","Both are open standards.","Runtime versions are irrelevant."]},{"q":"Which delegated-authentication method is still defensible today?","options":["ROPC for new web apps","Embedded webviews showing the IdP login","Any method, if TLS is used","Credential forwarding for legacy protocols with no browser, such as IMAP or LDAP clients"],"answer":3,"why":"Protocols with no browser cannot redirect. The mitigations are app-specific passwords or moving the protocol onto OAuth with SASL.","whyWrong":["ROPC is deprecated by the security BCP.","A webview lets the host app read what is typed, forwarding with extra steps.","TLS protects the wire, not the app holding the password.",""]},{"q":"Someone says \"we delegate authentication to Okta.\" What do they almost certainly mean?","options":["The app posts passwords to an Okta API","Okta issues them certificates","Okta stores their password file","Federation, the user authenticates at Okta and the app receives a signed assertion"],"answer":3,"why":"Both are literally delegated authentication, but the redirect style is what people mean, and it is the one that keeps the app out of the blast radius.","whyWrong":["That would be credential forwarding, which is the pattern to avoid.","Certificates are a different mechanism.","A password file is not how any of this works.",""]}],"idfdauthz":[{"q":"Before OAuth, an app needing your cloud photos asked for your cloud password. Which problem does delegated authorization NOT solve?","options":["Access that never expires","No way to revoke one app","Unlimited scope of access","The need to authenticate at all"],"answer":3,"why":"Delegation bounds, expires and revokes app access, but somebody still has to authenticate the user first.","whyWrong":["exp expires it.","Revoking the grant removes one app.","Scopes bound it.",""]},{"q":"What is the difference between revoking a token and revoking a grant?","options":["A token is one credential; the grant is the recorded permission, so revoking it also stops refreshes","Tokens outlive grants, so revoking the grant leaves issued tokens working until they lapse","They are identical, since revoking either one invalidates the same underlying authorization record","Grants cannot be revoked at all, so the only recourse is to wait for the tokens to expire"],"answer":0,"why":"\"Remove this app access\" means deleting the grant, otherwise the refresh token quietly mints new access tokens.","whyWrong":["","Grants outlive tokens, not the reverse.","They operate at different levels.","Grants are exactly what the user removes."]},{"q":"A token carries scope photos:read. Ada uses it to request Bob photos. What should happen?","options":["Denied, because a scope bounds the app but the user rights still apply","Allowed, the scope is present","Allowed if Bob account is public","Denied, because photos:read is read-only"],"answer":0,"why":"Both checks must pass: what the app was allowed to ask for, and what the user is actually allowed to see. Treating a scope as the whole decision is a real vulnerability.","whyWrong":["","The scope is necessary, not sufficient.","Ownership rules are not waived by a scope.","Read-only is not the reason; the reason is ownership."]},{"q":"What should a resource server do when the scope claim is missing or unparseable?","options":["Allow the request","Deny the request","Assume the default scope","Retry without the token"],"answer":1,"why":"Fail closed. An absent scope claim means no permissions, never full permissions.","whyWrong":["Failing open is how absent claims become privilege escalation.","","There is no safe default to assume.","Retrying unauthenticated is worse."]},{"q":"Why is accepting an OAuth access token as proof of who the user is exploitable?","options":["Access tokens are unsigned by design, so anything can forge one and claim to be any user it likes","It authorises an app to call an API and makes no reliable statement about the user, and may have been obtained for someone else","They expire far too quickly to be relied on, so the session breaks before the identity can be established","They are large enough to overflow header limits, so proxies truncate them and the identity claim is lost"],"answer":1,"why":"OAuth answers \"may this app do this?\" OpenID Connect adds the ID token to answer \"who is this?\"","whyWrong":["Many are signed; signing does not make them identity statements.","","Expiry is unrelated.","Size is irrelevant."]}],"idfobo":[{"q":"A gateway forwards the user original token unchanged to a downstream service. What breaks?","options":["The token gets too large","The signature becomes invalid","Nothing, this is standard","Every service must ignore the audience check, so one token now works everywhere"],"answer":3,"why":"The aud names the first service. Accepting it downstream means abandoning audience validation, which turns the token into a universal key.","whyWrong":["Forwarding does not change the size.","The signature is still valid, that is what makes it tempting.","It is a common shortcut and a known anti-pattern.",""]},{"q":"What distinguishes delegation from impersonation in a token?","options":["Impersonation tokens are shorter","They are the same","Delegation is encrypted","Delegation records the acting party in an act claim; impersonation shows only the user"],"answer":3,"why":"With an act claim the downstream can see \"service X acting for user Y\". Impersonation loses the acting party entirely.","whyWrong":["Size is not the distinction.","Token exchange formalises them as distinct modes.","Encryption is unrelated.",""]},{"q":"What is the may_act claim for?","options":["Naming who is permitted to act for this subject","Setting the token expiry","Listing the allowed scopes","Naming the issuer"],"answer":0,"why":"may_act gates delegation: it names which party may exchange this token to act on the subject behalf.","whyWrong":["","That is exp.","That is scope.","That is iss."]},{"q":"Why is passing X-User-Id: ada between services not an acceptable substitute for a token?","options":["It is unauthenticated, so any service that can reach the endpoint can claim to be anyone","It is slower than a token, because the receiver has to look the user up on every request","Headers are size-limited, so a user identifier gets truncated once the request grows","Headers are not logged by most proxies, so you lose the audit trail for the whole call"],"answer":0,"why":"A token is signed, so the downstream verifies the claim instead of trusting the caller. An unauthenticated header makes security depend entirely on perfect network isolation.","whyWrong":["","It is faster, and that is exactly the trap.","Size is not the problem.","Logging is not the problem either."]},{"q":"In a four-hop chain, what must be true of the token at each hop?","options":["Each hop should mint its own unsigned token","It must be the same token throughout","Only the last hop needs a token","Each hop needs its own audience, while the subject survives and the acting party is recorded"],"answer":3,"why":"Audience-per-hop preserves the point of audience validation; the subject and act claims preserve authorization and attribution.","whyWrong":["Unsigned tokens are not tokens.","That is the forwarding anti-pattern.","Every hop is a checkpoint.",""]}],"idfactas":[{"q":"What is the core modelling mistake in a \"view as this user\" feature?","options":["Collapsing the two subjects, minting the customer a session instead of keeping both identities","Not encrypting the session, so the impersonated identity is visible to anything inspecting traffic","Making it too slow, since each action has to be re-authorized against the impersonated account","Using cookies to carry the impersonated identity, rather than a claim inside the token itself"],"answer":0,"why":"With one subject the audit log says the customer acted. Keeping the authenticated subject and the effective subject separate is what makes everything else possible.","whyWrong":["","Encryption does not restore attribution.","Performance is not the risk.","Cookies are fine as a transport."]},{"q":"What permissions should an acting-as session have?","options":["The intersection of both, and usually less","The target permissions","The union of both","Whatever the engineer normally has"],"answer":0,"why":"If acting-as adopted the target rights, acting as an admin would hand the engineer admin. Intersect, then narrow further.","whyWrong":["","That is the escalation path.","A union is strictly worse.","Then it would not show what the customer sees."]},{"q":"Which action should stay denied in an acting-as session even when writes have been approved?","options":["Viewing configuration flags","Viewing an order","Changing the account email address","Reading a support note"],"answer":2,"why":"Changing email, changing the password, adding MFA factors, deleting the account and exporting data are all account-takeover primitives.","whyWrong":["Also a read.","A read, and the normal case.","","Also a read."]},{"q":"Why must an acting-as session show a persistent banner?","options":["To slow the operator down deliberately, so that destructive actions require a moment of conscious thought","Because the specification requires a visible indicator whenever a session carries a delegation claim","Because engineers forget which window they are in and act on production data believing it is their own account","To satisfy branding requirements, since the support tool must look visually distinct from the customer product"],"answer":2,"why":"It is a human-factors control, and a cheap one relative to the mistake it prevents.","whyWrong":["Friction is not the goal.","No spec mandates it; the risk does.","","Not a branding matter."]},{"q":"A customer reports a rendering bug. Which option should you reach for first?","options":["Impersonate them silently, so the support work does not clutter their notification feed","Full write acting-as, so you can reproduce the problem and fix it inside one session","A diagnostics or redacted view showing their config and screens without their content","Ask them for their password so you can sign in and see exactly what they are seeing"],"answer":2,"why":"Take the lowest rung of the ladder that solves the problem, and if it fits, ask the user to screen-share, which is consent given directly.","whyWrong":["Silent impersonation destroys attribution.","The highest-risk option for a read-only problem.","","Never ask for a password."]}],"idfzt":[{"q":"What did the perimeter model treat as authorization?","options":["A password","A certificate","A signed token","Being inside the network"],"answer":3,"why":"Authenticate once at the edge, then trust anything inside. One phished laptop puts the attacker inside, where nothing checks anything.","whyWrong":["A password was the edge check, not the interior one.","Certificates came later.","Tokens are the zero-trust answer, not the perimeter one.",""]},{"q":"\"Never trust, always verify\", what does always mean here?","options":["Once per day","Every request","Once per login","Once per session"],"answer":1,"why":"Every request re-establishes who is asking and whether they may. Signed, audience-scoped, short-lived tokens are what make that cheap.","whyWrong":["Daily is not verification.","","That is the perimeter model.","Per-session is still a long window for a revoked user."]},{"q":"A request arrives from 10.0.0.5, an internal address. How much should that contribute to the access decision?","options":["It should be denied outright","It should skip authentication","It should be trusted as identity","It is context at most, never identity"],"answer":3,"why":"An IP is a routing detail that can be spoofed, reassigned or shared. Context is evidence feeding a decision, never a reason to skip one.","whyWrong":["Internal addresses are not inherently suspicious either.","Skipping authentication is the failure mode.","That is precisely the perimeter assumption.",""]},{"q":"The policy decision point is unreachable. What should the enforcement point do?","options":["Deny","Permit reads only","Permit, to avoid an outage","Cache the last decision indefinitely"],"answer":0,"why":"Fail closed. A system that fails open under load is one an attacker will deliberately overload.","whyWrong":["","Reads still disclose data, so this is not a safe middle ground.","Failing open to protect availability hands an attacker a way in: overload the policy engine, then walk through.","Indefinite caching is failing open slowly, revoked access keeps working."]},{"q":"Which statement about zero trust is accurate?","options":["It is an architecture where identity, not network location, is what access decisions are made on","It means multi-factor authentication everywhere, applied uniformly to every user and service","It means trusting nothing at all, so every request is denied unless explicitly pre-approved","It is a product you can buy, sold as a gateway that enforces the model across your estate"],"answer":0,"why":"Trust is still granted constantly, it is just explicit, narrow, evidenced and short-lived rather than implied by a network cable.","whyWrong":["","MFA at the edge with a flat interior is still the perimeter model.","The name is unhelpful; verification, not absence of trust, is the point.","Vendors sell components, not the architecture."]}],"idfapikey":[{"q":"What is an API key, precisely?","options":["An encrypted password","A short-lived session cookie","A long-lived opaque bearer token identifying an application","A signed token identifying a user"],"answer":2,"why":"Every word matters: long-lived, opaque, bearer, and it names an application rather than a person.","whyWrong":["It is not derived from a password.","Sessions expire; keys usually never do.","","There is no user in the picture at all."]},{"q":"Why should an API key never travel in a URL query string?","options":["Query strings are not encrypted by TLS","It is slower than a header","Query strings have a length limit","URLs land in access logs, browser history and Referer headers sent to third parties"],"answer":3,"why":"A key in a URL has already been copied into infrastructure that assumes URLs are not secret.","whyWrong":["TLS does encrypt the URL in transit; the leak is at the endpoints.","Performance is irrelevant.","Length is not the issue.",""]},{"q":"Why give every issued key an identifiable prefix such as sk_live_?","options":["It encrypts the key","Secret scanners can spot a leaked key in a public repo and alert you","It is required by the HTTP spec","It makes the key longer and harder to brute force"],"answer":1,"why":"The prefix costs nothing and turns a silent leak into an alert, and it tells an engineer at a glance what they are holding.","whyWrong":["A prefix does not encrypt anything.","","No spec requires it.","A prefix is public and adds no entropy."]},{"q":"How should API keys be stored on the server?","options":["In plaintext, so support can read them out","Hashed, like passwords","In an environment variable","Encrypted with a reversible key"],"answer":1,"why":"Show the plaintext once at creation and store only a hash, so the database never contains a usable credential.","whyWrong":["If support can read it, so can an attacker with the same access.","","That is where the client keeps it, not the issuer.","Reversible encryption means a database breach is still a credential breach."]},{"q":"Where are API keys genuinely the wrong tool?","options":["Anywhere a user is involved, or where the code runs somewhere the user can read it","A nightly CI job, where no interactive user is present to complete an authorization flow","An internal script, where the caller is trusted and the blast radius is deliberately small","A webhook receiver, where the sender needs a stable credential it can configure once"],"answer":0,"why":"A key cannot represent a user, and a key shipped in a mobile app or SPA bundle is not a secret at all.","whyWrong":["","Also a good fit, though federation is better still.","Also a good fit.","A classic good fit."]}],"idfcapurl":[{"q":"What defines a capability URL?","options":["It uses a special HTTP method","The URL itself is the credential, holding the link is the authorization","It is very long","It requires a session cookie"],"answer":1,"why":"No session and no token header: possession of the unguessable link is the whole authorization.","whyWrong":["It is an ordinary GET or POST.","","Length is incidental.","If it needed a session it would not be a capability URL."]},{"q":"A password reset link is consumed before the user ever clicks it. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A corporate mail security gateway fetched the link to scan it","The token expired","The email was encrypted","The user clicked twice"],"answer":0,"why":"Scanners visit links to check for malware, which consumes a single-use GET. Requiring a POST to consume the token avoids it.","whyWrong":["","Expiry produces a different error, not a consumed token.","Encryption does not follow links.","Possible but far less common than scanning."]},{"q":"Why is Referrer-Policy relevant to capability URLs?","options":["If the page links to or loads anything external, the full capability URL can be sent to that third party","It compresses the URL so that the capability token stays within the length limits proxies enforce","It expires the link after a fixed interval, so a leaked capability stops working on its own","It signs the URL, letting the recipient verify that the capability was issued by the expected party"],"answer":0,"why":"The Referer header carries the whole URL, secret included. Set no-referrer on capability pages.","whyWrong":["","It does no compression.","Expiry is a server concern.","It provides no signature."]},{"q":"What is the trade-off between a stored capability and a signed one?","options":["Stored is revocable but needs a lookup; signed is stateless but cannot be revoked before expiry","Stored links cannot expire, since the record has no natural lifetime once it has been written","There is no meaningful difference, since both end up as an opaque string in the same URL","Signed links are always more secure, since the signature cannot be stripped or replayed by anyone"],"answer":0,"why":"It is the opaque-versus-structured token trade-off appearing again, this time in a URL.","whyWrong":["","Stored links expire perfectly well.","The revocation behaviour differs sharply.","Neither is universally more secure; they fail differently."]},{"q":"What should be logged when a capability URL is used?","options":["The full URL, for debugging","Only a short prefix identifying which capability was used","Nothing at all","The user password"],"answer":1,"why":"A prefix is enough to correlate the use in an audit trail without turning your logs into a credential store.","whyWrong":["That copies a working credential into your logs.","","You still need an audit trail.","Passwords are not involved."]}],"idfassume":[{"q":"A role has two policies. What does the trust policy control?","options":["Which region it works in","What the role can do once assumed","How long the credential lasts","Who is allowed to assume the role"],"answer":3,"why":"The trust policy is an identity question; the permission policy is the authorization question.","whyWrong":["Not a policy concern.","That is the permission policy.","Duration is a separate parameter.",""]},{"q":"A SaaS vendor is trusted by many customers. Why is an external id needed?","options":["To encrypt the request payload, so that the assumed credentials cannot be read in transit by the vendor","To speed up assumption, by letting the provider skip a lookup it would otherwise perform per call","Because being trusted by everyone means any customer could otherwise aim the vendor at another customer role","To identify the vendor, since the trust policy needs to know which principal is making the request"],"answer":2,"why":"The confused deputy: customer B names A role, and the vendor account genuinely is trusted by A, so the call succeeds. A per-customer secret breaks it.","whyWrong":["It provides no encryption.","It has no performance role.","","The account id already does that."]},{"q":"A cloud trust policy names the CI provider as OIDC issuer but places no condition on the sub claim. What is the consequence?","options":["Any repository on that CI platform can assume the role","Nothing, the issuer check is sufficient","Only private repositories can assume it","The role cannot be assumed at all"],"answer":0,"why":"The issuer check proves the token is real; the subject check proves it is the right one. You need both, exactly as with iss and aud.","whyWrong":["","The issuer vouches for every one of its tenants, not just yours.","Visibility is irrelevant to the trust policy.","It can be assumed, that is the problem."]},{"q":"What does workload identity federation remove?","options":["The need for any authorization decision, since the workload is already inside the network","The audit trail, because federated calls are attributed to the platform and not the caller","The role itself, since permissions attach directly to the workload's own identity instead","The long-lived static credential that role assumption otherwise needs to start from"],"answer":3,"why":"The workload exchanges the platform identity it already has for a role, so no secret is stored anywhere.","whyWrong":["Authorization still applies through the role permissions.","Auditing improves, since the original identity is recorded.","The role remains; only the bootstrap secret disappears.",""]},{"q":"After assuming a role, what should the audit log record?","options":["Only the role","Only the original caller","Both the original identity and the role assumed","Neither, it is anonymous"],"answer":2,"why":"Like the act claim, recording both preserves the answer to \"who did this?\" across the hop.","whyWrong":["Then attribution to a person is lost.","Then you cannot tell what permissions were used.","","Assumption is always attributable."]}],"az6":[{"q":"A role check passes and the handler returns repo.findById(id). What is the flaw?","options":["No object-level check, changing the id returns another customer record","The id should be a string","The role check is wrong","The query is slow"],"answer":0,"why":"This is IDOR, or broken object-level authorization: endpoint authorization passed, ownership was never checked.","whyWrong":["","The type is irrelevant.","The role check is fine and passed.","Performance is not the issue."]},{"q":"Does replacing sequential ids with UUIDs fix IDOR?","options":["No, it is obscurity, not authorization; references leak constantly","Yes, they cannot be guessed","Yes, if they are version 4","Only for public APIs"],"answer":0,"why":"Unguessable ids stop casual enumeration, but references appear in links, logs, exports and tickets. Do both, and never let the first substitute for the second.","whyWrong":["","Guessing is only one way to obtain a reference.","The version does not change the argument.","The argument applies everywhere."]},{"q":"Where should the tenant constraint live?","options":["In the UI","In the query itself, so the wrong tenant simply finds nothing","In the load balancer","In a check after the fetch"],"answer":1,"why":"Check-after fails the moment somebody adds a new query, and somebody always does. A repository that cannot fetch across tenants cannot leak across tenants.","whyWrong":["The UI is not an authorization boundary.","","A proxy does not know who owns record 4417.","It works until the next call site forgets."]},{"q":"Why can a gateway not enforce object-level authorization?","options":["It would break TLS","It is too slow","It does not know who owns record 4417, only the service holding the data does","Gateways cannot read tokens"],"answer":2,"why":"Coarse checks at the edge, ownership checks next to the data. A design that pushes all authorization to the perimeter has no answer to IDOR.","whyWrong":["TLS is unrelated.","Speed is not the limitation.","","Gateways read tokens perfectly well."]},{"q":"An API returns the full entity and the UI hides the salary field. What is wrong?","options":["The JSON already contains it and anyone can open developer tools","The field should be renamed","It should be cached","Nothing, the user cannot see it"],"answer":0,"why":"Mask or omit at serialisation, driven by the caller permissions. The UI is not an authorization boundary.","whyWrong":["","Renaming hides nothing.","Caching makes it worse.","They can see it in the network tab in seconds."]}],"az7":[{"q":"What is the difference between a group and a role?","options":["Groups are only in LDAP","A group is a collection of people; a role is a collection of permissions","They are the same","A role is bigger"],"answer":1,"why":"\"Who is in Finance?\" is an HR question; \"what may Finance do?\" is a security question. Collapsing them means org-chart changes become unreviewed permission changes.","whyWrong":["Groups exist in every identity system.","","They are administered differently and reviewed differently.","Size is not the distinction."]},{"q":"Why must a nested-group walk track visited nodes?","options":["To count members accurately, since a user reachable by two paths would otherwise be counted twice","For performance, since revisiting a group that has already been expanded wastes directory calls","Because nothing prevents a cycle, and a naive recursive walk would never terminate","To sort the output, since the traversal order determines how the resulting members are listed"],"answer":2,"why":"Group graphs can contain cycles, so the traversal needs a visited set. It is a graph problem, and it is where hand-rolled code fails.","whyWrong":["Counting does not require it.","Performance is a side benefit, not the reason.","","Ordering is unrelated."]},{"q":"How does most accidental over-permissioning happen with groups?","options":["Tokens expire late, so a permission removed yesterday remains usable until the token lapses","Someone grants a permission directly to a user, bypassing the group structure entirely","Someone nests a group under a broader parent, silently granting its access to everyone above","Passwords are shared between colleagues, so one person's access becomes several people's access"],"answer":2,"why":"No permission was ever granted, the shape of the tree changed, and inheritance did the rest.","whyWrong":["Unrelated.","Direct grants are visible and reviewable.","","Unrelated to group structure."]},{"q":"Long-tenured employees intermittently get 431 responses after login. What is the likely cause?","options":["Their password is too long","Their account is locked","Clock skew","Token bloat, every group emitted as a claim overflows proxy header limits"],"answer":3,"why":"Users in hundreds of groups produce oversized headers. Emit only groups relevant to the audience, send ids rather than names, or look them up at the API.","whyWrong":["Passwords are not in the token.","A lock produces a clear error.","Skew causes expiry errors, not 431s.",""]},{"q":"What number should an access review report for a person?","options":["Their last login date, which shows whether any of the access is being used at all","Their direct group memberships, which is the only thing a reviewer can actually act on","Their effective permissions, the flattened union across all groups, nested and direct","The number of groups they belong to, as a proxy for how much access they have picked up"],"answer":2,"why":"If you cannot produce that on demand you cannot answer the only question an auditor will ask.","whyWrong":["Activity is a different question.","Direct membership hides everything inherited.","","A count says nothing about access."]}],"az8":[{"q":"Under deny-overrides, two policies permit and one denies. What is the result?","options":["It depends on order","Permit, by majority","Not applicable","Deny"],"answer":3,"why":"A prohibition should not be defeatable by adding another rule somewhere else. This is the safe default.","whyWrong":["Ordering matters for first-applicable, not this.","Policy combination is not a vote.","Some policies did apply.",""]},{"q":"Why is permit-overrides risky as a general setting?","options":["It is not standardised","One careless broad grant silently undoes every restriction","It is slower","It cannot express deny"],"answer":1,"why":"Occasionally justified for break-glass, but as a default it means the weakest rule wins.","whyWrong":["It is a standard algorithm.","","Performance is identical.","It can express deny; deny just loses."]},{"q":"What must happen when no policy matches a request?","options":["Ask the user","Permit","Log and permit","Deny"],"answer":3,"why":"Default deny. Default permit means every resource nobody wrote a rule for is public, and you find out from the outside.","whyWrong":["Not a decision procedure.","That is the failure mode.","Logging does not make it safe.",""]},{"q":"Why keep \"not applicable\" distinct from \"deny\" inside the engine?","options":["It distinguishes \"a rule deliberately blocked this\" from \"no rule covered this\", very different bugs","To reduce log volume, since only genuine denials need to be recorded for later review","It runs faster, since a three-valued result can be short-circuited earlier than a boolean one","The specification mandates three values, so an implementation returning two would not be conformant"],"answer":0,"why":"Both collapse to denied at the enforcement point, but they need different fixes.","whyWrong":["","It adds detail rather than reducing it.","No performance difference.","Nothing requires it; it is good practice."]},{"q":"What is the most valuable output of a policy engine, beyond the decision itself?","options":["The engine version","Which policy decided","The response time","The number of policies evaluated"],"answer":1,"why":"Without it, debugging is guesswork, and the standard response to an unexplained denial is to add a broad permit until it works.","whyWrong":["Irrelevant to the decision.","","Useful for ops, not for correctness.","A count explains nothing."]}],"am4b":[{"q":"What makes a method phishing-resistant?","options":["Expiring within thirty seconds, leaving too little time for an attacker to relay it to the real site","The authenticator itself checks who is asking, because the origin is part of the cryptographic operation","A longer code, since more digits make interception and reuse within the validity window impractical","Sending it over TLS, so an attacker positioned on the network cannot read the secret in transit"],"answer":1,"why":"If the factor is something the user can read out and type, they can be induced to read it to an attacker. A code has no idea which site asked.","whyWrong":["A proxy relays within the window.","","Length does not help against relay.","TLS protects the wire, not the destination."]},{"q":"An attacker sends repeated push approvals at 3am until the user taps to make it stop. What is the defence?","options":["Longer push timeouts","More frequent pushes","Switching to SMS","Number matching, the user must type digits shown on the login screen"],"answer":3,"why":"Number matching defeats blind approval outright, because the user must read something from the real login screen.","whyWrong":["A longer window helps the attacker.","That worsens the fatigue.","SMS is weaker still.",""]},{"q":"A user has a passkey enrolled and also SMS as a fallback. How strong is the account?","options":["As strong as the passkey","As strong as SMS","The average of the two","Stronger than either alone"],"answer":1,"why":"The weakest enrolled method is the account real strength, because an attacker will always claim to have lost the strong one.","whyWrong":["Only if the weak fallback is removed.","","Security does not average.","Adding a weak path never strengthens."]},{"q":"Why should a biometric never be sent to a server?","options":["It is a password you can never change, and it cannot be revoked","It is too large","Servers cannot store images","It would be slow"],"answer":0,"why":"A biometric is a local gesture that unlocks a key on the device, not a factor you transmit.","whyWrong":["","Size is not the concern.","They can, which is the danger.","Speed is irrelevant."]},{"q":"Which is the most common route to taking over an account protected by strong MFA?","options":["Guessing the PIN","The account recovery flow","Cloning the security key","Breaking the cryptography"],"answer":1,"why":"A hardware key protecting an account whose recovery is an emailed link is protected by email.","whyWrong":["PINs are rate-limited by the authenticator.","","Cloning is impractical.","Nobody breaks the cryptography."]}],"am4c":[{"q":"What are the two specifications that make up FIDO2?","options":["TLS and X.509","SAML and XML-DSig","OAuth and OIDC","WebAuthn and CTAP2"],"answer":3,"why":"WebAuthn is the browser API your code calls; CTAP2 is how the browser talks to an external authenticator.","whyWrong":["Transport and certificates, not FIDO2.","A different federation stack.","Different family entirely.",""]},{"q":"A user signs in with Touch ID rather than a USB key. What changes on your server?","options":["Nothing, both present an identical WebAuthn surface","It must speak CTAP2","It must verify attestation","It must store a different key type"],"answer":0,"why":"A platform authenticator involves no CTAP at all, but the server sees the same ceremony either way, and generally should not care.","whyWrong":["","CTAP is between the browser and an external device.","Attestation is optional in both cases.","The key format is the same."]},{"q":"What does a discoverable (resident) credential enable?","options":["Longer keys","Offline verification","Usernameless login, because the authenticator knows which accounts it holds","Faster signatures"],"answer":2,"why":"Non-discoverable credentials wrap the key into the credential id, so the server must say which credentials to try, meaning the user identifies themselves first.","whyWrong":["Key length is unrelated.","Verification is offline either way.","","Signing speed is unchanged."]},{"q":"A page at login.example.com registers a credential with rpId \"com\". What happens?","options":["It silently becomes example.com","It works across all subdomains","It is rejected as a public suffix","It works but only on that host"],"answer":2,"why":"The RP ID must be the origin domain or a registrable suffix of it. A public suffix would scope the credential across an entire TLD.","whyWrong":["No silent correction occurs.","That is what example.com would do.","","Rejected, not narrowed."]},{"q":"Why choose the RP ID carefully before registering anyone?","options":["It affects signature speed, because a longer RP ID lengthens the data being signed","It determines the key algorithm the authenticator will pick when it mints the credential","It is baked into every credential and cannot be changed without re-registering every user","It appears in the browser prompt, so a poor choice confuses users during registration"],"answer":2,"why":"Pick the broadest domain you might ever need at the start, or you will invalidate every credential later.","whyWrong":["No performance effect.","Algorithms come from pubKeyCredParams.","","Users never see it."]}],"am8b":[{"q":"Why must the user handle not be an email address?","options":["Emails contain characters the encoding does not permit, so the handle would fail to round-trip","The handle must be numeric, since authenticators index credentials by an integer identifier","It is stored on the authenticator and may be displayed, so it would leak to anyone holding the device","Emails are too long, and the handle has a size limit that many real addresses would exceed"],"answer":2,"why":"The user handle should be an opaque, stable id you can key on, not a personal identifier.","whyWrong":["Encoding is not the problem.","It need not be numeric.","","Length is not the issue."]},{"q":"What is the difference between the UP and UV flags?","options":["UP is for platform authenticators only","UV means the server verified the user","UP means someone touched the device; UV means a PIN or biometric was checked locally","They are the same"],"answer":2,"why":"UP is one factor; UV makes the authenticator two factors on its own. Requesting userVerification is only a preference, you must check the bit.","whyWrong":["Both apply to either type.","Verification happens on the authenticator, not the server.","","They carry different meanings."]},{"q":"What do the BE and BS flags tell you?","options":["The battery state","The key algorithm","Whether the credential can be synced, and whether it currently is","The signature counter"],"answer":2,"why":"Backup eligible and backup state distinguish a synced passkey from a device-bound credential, and BE cannot change after registration.","whyWrong":["Not a hardware indicator.","Algorithms are in the COSE key.","","That is signCount."]},{"q":"Why is attestation: \"none\" a reasonable default?","options":["Browsers silently discard the attestation object anyway, so requesting it has no observable effect on the ceremony","Attestation is cryptographically unsound, so the specification recommends turning it off in production deployments","Omitting attestation produces a smaller response that the browser can parse and validate noticeably faster","Verifying it properly needs trust anchors and metadata, and phishing resistance comes from origin binding rather than knowing the brand"],"answer":3,"why":"It is genuinely useful only when an enterprise must enforce approved authenticator models. Otherwise it adds a privacy cost and no security benefit.","whyWrong":["Browsers pass it through.","It is secure, just usually unnecessary.","Parsing cost is trivial.",""]},{"q":"An origin check is written as origin.startsWith(\"https://login.example.com\"). What gets in?","options":["Nothing, it is safe","Only subdomains","Only HTTP origins","https://login.example.com.evil.com"],"answer":3,"why":"Origin validation must be exact set membership against an allow-list, never a prefix or contains comparison.","whyWrong":["It is a real and common vulnerability.","It admits far more than subdomains.","Scheme is not the issue.",""]}],"am8c":[{"q":"What does an empty allowCredentials list trigger?","options":["An error, since the specification requires at least one credential descriptor to be present","Usernameless login, the authenticator offers whatever discoverable credentials it holds","A fallback to password, since the browser has nothing to match against and offers the older method","Registration instead of login, since an empty list signals that no credential exists yet"],"answer":1,"why":"The authenticator returns a userHandle identifying who was chosen, which is why the handle must be a stable opaque id.","whyWrong":["It is a valid and deliberate request.","","No fallback is implied.","The ceremony type is set by the API call."]},{"q":"What exactly does the authenticator sign during authentication?","options":["authenticatorData concatenated with SHA-256 of clientDataJSON","The user password","The credential id","Only the challenge"],"answer":0,"why":"Because the browser writes the origin into clientDataJSON and its hash is inside the signed bytes, a phishing proxy cannot alter it.","whyWrong":["","No password is involved.","The credential id is not the signed payload.","The challenge alone would not bind the origin."]},{"q":"A synced passkey reports signCount 0 and your stored value is 0. What should you do?","options":["Increment the stored value","Accept it, counters are unsupported, which is normal for synced passkeys","Force re-registration","Reject it as a cloned authenticator"],"answer":1,"why":"Treating 0 as a regression locks out every synced-passkey user. A genuine regression is a signal to log and investigate, not an automatic rejection.","whyWrong":["There is nothing to increment.","","Nothing is wrong.","That is the lockout bug."]},{"q":"You look up the stored public key by credential id and verify the signature. What else must you check?","options":["Nothing more","The key length","That the credential belongs to the account being logged into","The authenticator brand"],"answer":2,"why":"Otherwise a credential valid for Bob authenticates Ada, an account-confusion bug.","whyWrong":["One step is missing.","Length is fixed by the algorithm.","","The brand is irrelevant."]},{"q":"WebAuthn is very hard to attack directly. Where do realistic attacks go instead?","options":["The USB protocol","The signature algorithm","The random challenge","Recovery, enrollment, downgrade and post-login session theft"],"answer":3,"why":"The cryptography holds, so everything depends on the flows around it, and a login that was unphishable still produces an ordinary bearer cookie.","whyWrong":["CTAP is not the soft spot.","ES256 is not the weak point.","A correctly generated challenge is not attackable.",""]}],"ao4b":[{"q":"Which check actually makes a token sender-constrained under DPoP?","options":["Comparing the thumbprint of that JWK to cnf.jkt in the access token","Checking that iat is recent","Verifying the proof signature with the JWK in its own header","Checking the htu value"],"answer":0,"why":"Without it the proof shows only that someone owns some key. Comparing to cnf.jkt is what ties the key to this token.","whyWrong":["","That limits the replay window only.","Necessary but circular on its own, it proves the sender holds that key, not that the token was issued to it.","That stops endpoint replay, not token theft."]},{"q":"What are htm and htu for in a DPoP proof?","options":["Identifying the client, since the proof must name which application generated the key pair","Encrypting the request, since both fields feed the derivation of the transport-level key","Naming the audience, so the resource server can confirm the proof was intended for itself","Binding the proof to this HTTP method and URI, so a captured proof cannot be reused elsewhere"],"answer":3,"why":"A proof captured against one endpoint is useless against another.","whyWrong":["The jwk identifies the key, not the client.","DPoP signs, it does not encrypt.","That is the token aud claim.",""]},{"q":"Why do server-provided DPoP nonces exist when a replay cache already works?","options":["They lengthen the proof so that it carries enough entropy to resist offline guessing of the key thumbprint","They replace the proof signature entirely, so the server can validate freshness without any asymmetric cryptography","They are cheaper to compute than a full replay cache lookup, so the server avoids a round trip to shared storage","A replay cache needs shared state across a fleet; a nonce lets the server choose the value with no per-request storage"],"answer":3,"why":"Shared jti state is exactly what stateless tokens were meant to avoid, and a nonce cannot be minted in advance.","whyWrong":["Length is irrelevant.","The signature is still required.","Computation is not the issue.",""]},{"q":"Why must the refresh token be constrained too?","options":["Otherwise a stolen refresh token simply mints new access tokens, making the constraint pointless","It is larger, so an unconstrained refresh token is more attractive to an attacker who finds one","Refresh tokens expire faster than access tokens, so the binding has to be re-established often","The specification forbids bearer refresh tokens everywhere, regardless of the client type"],"answer":0,"why":"Constraining access tokens while leaving refresh bearer protects nothing.","whyWrong":["","Size is irrelevant.","They live far longer.","It is required for DPoP public clients specifically."]},{"q":"When is mTLS-bound preferable to DPoP?","options":["In service-to-service and regulated environments where a PKI already exists","In a browser app","Whenever CDNs are involved","For mobile apps"],"answer":0,"why":"mTLS is hardware-backed and harder to steal, but needs a PKI and TLS terminated where the certificate is still visible.","whyWrong":["","Client certificates effectively do not work in browsers.","Client certificates often break through proxies and CDNs.","DPoP suits mobile far better."]}],"oa8b":[{"q":"Why is storing an access token in localStorage discouraged?","options":["It is not encrypted at rest, so anyone with disk access can read it out of the browser profile","Browsers are deprecating it, and it will stop being reachable from page scripts entirely","It is too small to hold a signed token once the claim set grows past a handful of fields","Any JavaScript on the page can read it, so one XSS or one bad dependency exfiltrates it"],"answer":3,"why":"If your app can read the token, so can injected script. No care with the flow changes that.","whyWrong":["Encryption would not help, since the app must decrypt it to use it.","localStorage is not being removed.","Capacity is not the issue.",""]},{"q":"What does a BFF actually eliminate?","options":["Cross-site scripting, since the browser no longer executes any application-supplied code","Cross-site request forgery, since the cookie is no longer sent on cross-origin navigations","The durable credential in the browser, so an XSS cannot grant lasting access","The need for a session, since the front end can authenticate each request independently"],"answer":2,"why":"Injected script can still act as the user while the page is open, but there is no token to steal and reuse from elsewhere.","whyWrong":["XSS remains possible.","A BFF reintroduces CSRF, because the browser now authenticates with a cookie.","","It creates one."]},{"q":"Adopting a BFF reintroduces which problem?","options":["Clock skew","CSRF, because the browser authenticates with a cookie","Token theft","Weak passwords"],"answer":1,"why":"SameSite cookies plus a per-session CSRF token on state-changing requests is the standard answer.","whyWrong":["Unrelated.","","That is what the BFF removes.","Unrelated."]},{"q":"A team genuinely cannot run any backend. What is the least-bad option?","options":["A short-lived access token in memory with PKCE and no refresh token in the browser","Refresh token in localStorage","A long-lived access token in a cookie readable by script","An API key in the bundle"],"answer":0,"why":"Exposed while the page is open, but nothing durable is left behind, and re-authentication on refresh is accepted.","whyWrong":["","The worst case: one XSS yields lasting access.","A script-readable cookie is no better than localStorage.","A key in a bundle is public."]},{"q":"Conceptually, what does a BFF turn a SPA back into?","options":["An identity provider, since it issues the session the browser subsequently presents","A public client, since the browser still initiates the flow and holds no durable secret","A resource server, since it now validates tokens rather than requesting them on its own behalf","A confidential client with a session, which is what server-rendered apps always were"],"answer":3,"why":"The industry spent a decade moving tokens into the browser and has spent recent years moving them back out.","whyWrong":["It does not authenticate users itself.","It stops being a public client (it now has a real secret.)","The BFF calls the API; it is not the API.",""]}],"oa11":[{"q":"What is OAuth 2.1, fundamentally?","options":["A consolidation that folds the Security BCP into the base spec and removes discouraged grants","A new protocol incompatible with OAuth 2.0","A replacement for OpenID Connect","A new token format"],"answer":0,"why":"It invents nothing. If you have been following the Security BCP you are already writing OAuth 2.1.","whyWrong":["","There is no protocol incompatibility.","OIDC still sits on top, unchanged.","Token formats are untouched."]},{"q":"Which grants does OAuth 2.1 remove?","options":["Only implicit","Client credentials and device code","Authorization code and refresh","Implicit and password (ROPC)"],"answer":3,"why":"Implicit returned a token in the URL fragment; ROPC has the app collect the password. Both are superseded.","whyWrong":["ROPC is removed too.","Both remain.","Both remain and are central.",""]},{"q":"What is the biggest practical change for a confidential client?","options":["PKCE becomes required, because it defends against code injection which a client secret does not address","It must stop using refresh tokens, which were removed in favour of re-authenticating the user","Nothing changes, since the requirements that were added apply only to public clients in practice","It must adopt DPoP, since bearer tokens are no longer permitted for any client type at all"],"answer":0,"why":"The old \"PKCE is for public clients\" guidance is obsolete.","whyWrong":["","Refresh tokens remain, with rotation or binding.","PKCE becomes mandatory.","Sender constraint is one option for refresh tokens, not a blanket requirement."]},{"q":"How must redirect URIs be matched?","options":["By regular expression","By prefix","By exact string comparison, with no wildcards","By domain only"],"answer":2,"why":"Loose redirect matching is one of the most reliable ways to steal an authorization code.","whyWrong":["A regex is worse still.","Prefix matching is precisely the vulnerability.","","Domain-only matching admits any path."]},{"q":"When auditing an existing deployment, where does the real risk usually sit?","options":["The key length, since the minimum acceptable size was raised for every signing algorithm","A legacy grant still enabled on the authorization server for one unmigrated client","The token format, since the profile changed what a conforming access token must contain","The scope names, since the profile renamed several of the values clients are expected to request"],"answer":1,"why":"The grants are removed from the specification, but servers keep supporting them for compatibility, and an enabled-but-unused grant is still enabled.","whyWrong":["Key length is unrelated.","","Formats are unchanged by 2.1.","Scope naming is not a 2.1 concern."]}],"oa2b":[{"q":"What was the original attack PKCE was designed to stop?","options":["Replay of the ID token, by binding it to a value only the original requester could produce","Password guessing, by requiring proof of the verifier before the token endpoint will respond","Authorization code interception, where another app registers the same custom URL scheme","Token expiry, by allowing a client to prove continuity without holding a long-lived secret"],"answer":2,"why":"A native app has no secret, so the authorization server could not tell the real app from a malicious one receiving the same redirect.","whyWrong":["That is what nonce addresses.","Unrelated to the code flow.","","Expiry is not an attack."]},{"q":"Why does PKCE now apply to confidential clients as well?","options":["Because client secrets leak from deployment configuration often enough that they can no longer be trusted alone","Because of code injection, where an attacker feeds the victim a code for the attacker account, a client secret does not help","To speed up the flow, since the verifier check replaces a slower round trip against the token endpoint","For consistency, so that every client type follows one flow and implementers have fewer variations to support"],"answer":1,"why":"The client is genuine; the code is foreign. The victim client holds a verifier that does not match the challenge the attacker code was bound to.","whyWrong":["That is a different problem.","","It adds a hash.","It is a real attack, not tidiness."]},{"q":"Why is it safe for the code_challenge to travel through the browser?","options":["The browser hides it","It is encrypted","It is short-lived","It is a SHA-256 hash, and the verifier cannot be recovered from it"],"answer":3,"why":"The challenge is public by design; the verifier travels only on the direct back-channel POST, once, at the end.","whyWrong":["URLs are highly visible.","It is hashed, not encrypted.","Lifetime is not what protects it.",""]},{"q":"What is the PKCE downgrade attack?","options":["Omitting code_verifier at the token endpoint and hoping the server treats it as optional","Using a shorter authorization code so the verifier can be brute-forced before it expires","Reusing a code challenge from an earlier authorization request that already completed","Forcing the plain challenge method instead of S256 so the verifier travels in the clear"],"answer":0,"why":"If a challenge was stored, the verifier must be required. Absence is failure, not a skipped optional check.","whyWrong":["","Code length is set by the server.","Reuse is a different flaw.","A related weakness, but the downgrade is the omission."]},{"q":"How do state and code_challenge differ?","options":["code_challenge replaces state entirely, so implementations should stop sending the older parameter","They are the same protection under two names, one from OAuth and one from the PKCE extension","state applies only to mobile clients, where the redirect can be intercepted by another app","state binds the response to the user session (CSRF); code_challenge binds the code to the client secret"],"answer":3,"why":"You need both, and in OIDC nonce is a third thing again, binding the ID token to this login.","whyWrong":["Neither substitutes for the other.","They defend different things.","state applies everywhere.",""]}],"jose7":[{"q":"What does the signed payload of an SD-JWT actually contain for a selectively-disclosable claim?","options":["Nothing at all","The claim value","A salted hash (digest) of the claim, with the value travelling separately","An encrypted claim"],"answer":2,"why":"The holder can drop disclosures without breaking the signature, because the payload never changes, only which disclosures accompany it.","whyWrong":["The digests are present, which is how the verifier checks what it receives.","That is an ordinary JWT, where dropping a claim breaks the signature.","","SD-JWT hashes rather than encrypts."]},{"q":"Why does every disclosure begin with random salt?","options":["Without it a verifier could brute-force a guessable value such as a birthdate until a hash matched","To make the disclosure longer, so that its length reveals nothing about the claim it contains","To identify the issuer, since the salt is derived from the issuer key and binds the two together","To set the expiry, since the salt encodes a timestamp the verifier checks before accepting it"],"answer":0,"why":"Salt makes the digest input unguessable, and unique salts also stop a verifier correlating the same value across presentations.","whyWrong":["","Length is not the goal.","The iss claim does that.","Unrelated to expiry."]},{"q":"What problem does the key-binding JWT solve?","options":["Selective disclosure, letting a holder reveal individual claims while keeping the remaining ones hidden","Signature verification, by carrying the issuer public key so a verifier needs no prior trust anchor","Revocation, since the binding lets an issuer withdraw a credential that has already been handed to a holder","Replay, it names this verifier and this nonce, so a captured presentation cannot be reused elsewhere or by someone else"],"answer":3,"why":"The issuer puts the holder public key in cnf, and the holder signs a small JWT proving they hold the matching private key.","whyWrong":["That is what the digests do.","The issuer signature is separate.","Revocation needs a separate status mechanism.",""]},{"q":"Two verifiers compare notes. Can they tell they saw the same SD-JWT credential?","options":["Yes, the issuer signature is identical in every presentation, so SD-JWT is not unlinkable","No, because each presentation reveals a different subset, so there is nothing to line up","Only if the holder reused a salt across both presentations of the same credential","Only with the issuer's private key, which no verifier is ever given access to"],"answer":0,"why":"Removing that linkage needs BBS+ or zero-knowledge proofs. SD-JWT deliberately trades unlinkability for being implementable with ordinary JOSE libraries.","whyWrong":["","The signature is the same regardless of which disclosures are sent.","Salt reuse makes it worse, but the signature already links them.","No key is needed to compare signatures."]},{"q":"A verifier receives a presentation with one disclosure. What does it learn about the withheld claims?","options":["Nothing at all, since a presentation carries only the disclosures the holder chose to send","That they exist, because the digests are still in _sd, though not what they are","Their values, because the issuer signs the full claim set into the payload before selection","Their names only, since the _sd array stores each withheld claim name in the clear"],"answer":1,"why":"An honest limitation: the verifier can see you withheld two things, which is occasionally itself informative.","whyWrong":["The digests remain visible in the payload.","","Digests cannot be inverted when salted.","Names are inside the disclosure, which was not sent."]}],"ss6":[{"q":"Why is Single Logout structurally hard?","options":["Logout messages are slow, so the browser times out before every relying party has been reached","One login silently created N independent app sessions, and there is no reliable way to reach them all","Browsers block logout requests, since they arrive as third-party traffic in a hidden frame","The specification is incomplete, leaving the ordering of the notifications undefined between parties"],"answer":1,"why":"Independent app sessions are what make federated login fast, and they are exactly what makes logout unreliable. You cannot have one without the other.","whyWrong":["Speed is not the issue.","","Browsers do not block it.","The specs exist; distributed state is the problem."]},{"q":"A user logs out of an app and is immediately logged back in on the next click. What happened?","options":["A bug in the cookie","Local logout ended the app session, but the IdP session was untouched","The token never expired","CSRF"],"answer":1,"why":"Three different things are called logout, local, IdP, and full SLO. Most complaints are a mismatch between which one the user expected.","whyWrong":["The cookie was cleared correctly.","","A new login occurred rather than an old token being reused.","Unrelated."]},{"q":"Why is front-channel logout in terminal decline?","options":["It is too slow, since each relying party has to be contacted in sequence before logout completes","It depends on an app cookie being sent inside a cross-site iframe, which browsers now block by default","It requires JavaScript, which some relying parties disable for the pages that handle session state","It was never standardised, so each identity provider implemented an incompatible variant of it"],"answer":1,"why":"Third-party cookie restrictions remove the mechanism it relies on, and it also fails when the user closes the tab.","whyWrong":["Speed is not the problem.","","Iframes work without scripting.","It is a published OIDC specification."]},{"q":"Why must a logout token not contain a nonce claim?","options":["It breaks the signature, because the nonce is computed after signing and cannot be covered by it","A nonce marks the JWT as an ID token, so accepting one would let an attacker submit an ID token as a logout token","It would make the token too large for the back channel, which has tighter size limits than a redirect","Nonces expire quickly, so a logout arriving late would be rejected for a reason unrelated to logout"],"answer":1,"why":"The specification forbids it precisely to keep the two token types distinguishable.","whyWrong":["The signature would still verify, which is the danger.","","Size is irrelevant.","Expiry is not the issue."]},{"q":"An app uses stateless JWT sessions. What happens when back-channel logout arrives?","options":["The JWT is rewritten in place by the identity provider, so the next request carries the revoked state with it","The user is redirected to the identity provider, which notices the missing session and clears the local cookie","There is nothing to delete, back-channel logout effectively requires server-side session state or a revocation list","The session ends normally, because the token contains a logout claim the resource server checks on each request"],"answer":2,"why":"The app must find and kill the session from sid or sub alone, which means indexing sessions somewhere.","whyWrong":["A JWT cannot be modified after issuance.","No browser is involved.","","A stateless session cannot be ended by deleting a record."]},{"q":"Given SLO is unreliable, which single measure most reduces post-logout access?","options":["More iframes","Clearing localStorage","Revoking the refresh token and the grant, plus short access token lifetimes","A longer logout timeout"],"answer":2,"why":"Without revoking the grant, a refresh quietly mints a new access token. Logging out does not un-issue tokens already handed out.","whyWrong":["More notifications do not fix the unreachable ones.","Helps locally, not for issued tokens.","","Timeouts do not shorten token validity."]}],"ao7":[{"q":"What does a security profile such as FAPI actually do?","options":["Removes optionality, turning shoulds into MUSTs and picking one option where the base spec offered several","It adds a new grant type intended for regulated sectors, alongside the ones the core specification defines","It adds new cryptography, defining algorithms that plain OAuth deployments are not permitted to use","It replaces OAuth with a distinct protocol that happens to reuse some of the same endpoint names"],"answer":0,"why":"Every FAPI requirement is a defence already available in OAuth. The contribution is refusing to let any of them be optional.","whyWrong":["","No new grants are defined.","It introduces no new primitives.","It constrains OAuth rather than replacing it."]},{"q":"Which client authentication methods does FAPI permit?","options":["HTTP Basic only","client_secret_basic and client_secret_post","private_key_jwt and mTLS","Any, including none"],"answer":2,"why":"Asymmetric methods have no shared secret, so there is nothing that can leak from either side.","whyWrong":["Basic uses a shared secret.","Shared secrets are exactly what is excluded.","","none is never acceptable here."]},{"q":"What does PAR contribute?","options":["Longer tokens, since the request object can carry claims that would not fit in a query string safely","Encrypted tokens, by requiring the client to protect the authorization request payload end to end","Request parameters go to the authorization server over the back channel, so the browser carries only a handle","Faster token issuance, because the authorization server can pre-validate parameters before the browser arrives"],"answer":2,"why":"Parameters never appear in a URL, so they cannot be tampered with, logged or leaked through a referrer.","whyWrong":["Unrelated to token size.","PAR does not encrypt tokens.","","It adds a round trip."]},{"q":"What distinguishes FAPI Message Signing from the baseline profile?","options":["Support for more grant types, extending the profile to flows the baseline excludes","Stronger encryption, since the profile mandates a larger key size throughout the deployment","Faster flows, since signing removes a validation round trip the baseline profile requires","Non-repudiation, a signed record of what each party sent, which survives the exchange"],"answer":3,"why":"It is a legal requirement rather than a cryptographic one, which is why it is a separate level rather than simply \"more secure\".","whyWrong":["Grant support is unchanged.","The cryptography is comparable.","Signing adds work.",""]},{"q":"A deployment is fully FAPI-certified. What does that not tell you?","options":["That tokens are sender-constrained, since the profile requires binding them to the client that requested them","That client authentication is strong, since the profile mandates private key JWT or mutual TLS throughout","Whether the resource server checks record ownership, protocol hardening and authorization correctness are different problems","That PKCE is in use, since the profile requires it on every authorization code flow without exception"],"answer":2,"why":"A certified deployment can still have an IDOR on its main endpoint. Only one of these two problems has a test suite.","whyWrong":["Certification does cover that.","Certification does cover that.","","Certification does cover that."]}],"run1":[{"q":"An access token is stolen. Why can you not simply revoke it?","options":["Only the user can revoke it","Revocation endpoints are optional","A self-contained token verifies offline, so there is nothing central to switch off","It is encrypted"],"answer":2,"why":"Your real lever is the short lifetime, which is why 5-15 minutes is an incident-response decision rather than a performance one.","whyWrong":["Revocation is a server-side action.","The endpoint exists; it just cannot recall an issued token.","","Encryption is unrelated to revocability."]},{"q":"Which compromise lets an attacker mint valid tokens with nothing in your authentication logs?","options":["A refresh token","The IdP signing key","A session cookie","A client secret"],"answer":1,"why":"No login occurs at all, the attacker forges tokens directly, so authentication logging shows nothing.","whyWrong":["That is one user, and revoking the grant stops it.","","One user, one app.","Serious, but scoped to that application."]},{"q":"During emergency key rotation, what determines your worst-case exposure window?","options":["The token lifetime","The number of users","The signing algorithm","The JWKS cache TTL at the relying parties"],"answer":3,"why":"You cannot force a refetch you do not control. A 24-hour cache means a 24-hour window in which forged tokens still verify.","whyWrong":["Relevant, but the stale key is the longer window.","Population size does not affect it.","Algorithm choice is unrelated.",""]},{"q":"You reset a compromised user password. Why might the attacker still have access?","options":["The reset mail was intercepted, so the attacker chose the new password before the user did","The reset takes time to propagate to every replica, so the old password keeps working","They enrolled their own authenticator, API key, client or federated trust during the session","Passwords are cached in the application tier and only refreshed on the next restart"],"answer":2,"why":"Hunting persistence is the step people skip. Enumerate every credential and trust attached to the account, and everything created during the window.","whyWrong":["Possible but not the structural reason.","Propagation is near-instant.","","Not how credential storage works."]},{"q":"What should you do before rotating anything during an incident?","options":["Snapshot the logs, because identity logs often have the shortest retention","Restart the IdP","Disable MFA to speed up recovery","Notify the whole company"],"answer":0,"why":"Rotation and restarts destroy the evidence you need to establish when the attacker got in.","whyWrong":["","A restart clears the foothold and the evidence together.","Never; that widens the compromise.","Communicate, but evidence first."]}],"run2":[{"q":"Why is a big-bang identity cutover rarely viable?","options":["Licensing forbids running two identity providers at once, so a phased approach is contractually impossible","Users dislike change, and a single disruptive event generates more support load than a gradual one","You cannot repoint hundreds of applications atomically, so every migration is a period of coexistence","It costs more, since running the old and new systems in parallel doubles the infrastructure bill"],"answer":2,"why":"The design question is not how to move but how to run both systems safely, often for a year.","whyWrong":["Licensing is not the constraint.","User sentiment is not the blocker.","","Cost is secondary to feasibility."]},{"q":"What is lazy password migration?","options":["Storing both hashes side by side, so either system can authenticate the user during the overlap","Delaying the project until the old system can be decommissioned in a single planned cutover","Importing old hashes as-is, then rehashing with the new algorithm on each successful login","Forcing everyone to reset their password, so no legacy hash ever reaches the new system"],"answer":2,"why":"Users notice nothing and the population migrates itself; after a cutoff you force a reset for whoever is left, usually inactive accounts.","whyWrong":["Nested hashing is a different technique.","Not a scheduling term.","","That is the option of last resort."]},{"q":"Why can passkeys generally not be migrated to a new IdP?","options":["They are bound to the RP ID, and a different RP ID means every user re-enrolls","The format is proprietary","They expire","They are stored server-side"],"answer":0,"why":"The RP ID is baked into the credential at registration and cannot be changed. Plan for re-enrollment, and use it to enroll something stronger.","whyWrong":["","WebAuthn is an open standard.","Passkeys do not expire.","The private key never leaves the device."]},{"q":"Users log in successfully after a migration but see empty accounts. What went wrong?","options":["MFA enrollment, since the second factor was re-registered against a different user record","Identifier mapping, apps stored the old identifier and the new IdP issues a different sub","TLS configuration, since the new endpoint presents a certificate the client silently rejects","The password migration, since the hashes were rewritten but the attribute mapping was not"],"answer":1,"why":"Build the mapping before cutover and assert the legacy identifier as an extra claim during the overlap. Never join on email.","whyWrong":["MFA is unrelated to account contents.","","Unrelated.","Authentication clearly worked."]},{"q":"Which application should you migrate first?","options":["The most important one, so the work gets executive attention and resourcing from the start","A low-traffic internal app, so the first real cutover teaches you what the runbook got wrong","The largest one, because getting the hardest integration done first de-risks everything after it","All of them together, so you only have to run the old and the new stack side by side once"],"answer":1,"why":"Migrate in order of recoverability, not importance. The crown jewels go last, when the process is boring.","whyWrong":["That maximises the cost of learning.","","Size is not the criterion.","That is a big bang."]}],"run3":[{"q":"The IdP is down. What typically still works?","options":["Only administrative access, because break-glass accounts are designed to bypass the IdP","Existing sessions, until they expire, so an outage looks small at first and grows","New logins keep working but refreshes fail, since only refresh talks to the IdP directly","Nothing, every request is checked against the IdP so the whole estate stops at the same moment"],"answer":1,"why":"That shape is why session lifetime is a continuity parameter as well as a security one.","whyWrong":["Admins are affected like everyone else.","","New logins are exactly what fails.","Sessions already established are unaffected."]},{"q":"A verifier cannot reach the JWKS endpoint. What should it do?","options":["Accept all tokens","Serve stale cached keys and keep verifying","Retry until it succeeds","Reject all tokens"],"answer":1,"why":"A verifier that hard-fails on an unreachable JWKS turns an IdP blip into a total outage of every API.","whyWrong":["Never accept unverified tokens.","","Retrying does not answer the current request.","That is the amplification to avoid."]},{"q":"What is the recursive failure that makes break-glass accounts useless?","options":["They are too powerful, so the controls placed around them make routine testing prohibitively risky","Nobody knows they exist, since the people who created them have usually moved on by the time they matter","They expire quietly, and nobody notices until the credential is needed and has already lapsed","Their credentials live behind the thing they exist to bypass, a password manager or wiki behind SSO"],"answer":3,"why":"Also check where the alert goes: a chat tool behind SSO cannot be read during the incident.","whyWrong":["Strength is not a failure mode.","A documentation issue, not a structural one.","Expiry is a real problem, but not the recursive one.",""]},{"q":"How often should break-glass accounts be tested?","options":["Never, since exercising the account creates exactly the exposure the controls around it exist to prevent","Only after an incident, when the procedure is fresh and the gaps it revealed are still well understood","Once at creation, to confirm the credential works, after which the sealed record should not be disturbed","Regularly, an untested one has usually expired, been disabled by a cleanup script, or lost its password"],"answer":3,"why":"An untested emergency procedure is a hope, not a control.","whyWrong":["Untested means unavailable when needed.","That is too late.","Estates change around it.",""]},{"q":"What is the tension between short sessions and continuity?","options":["Short sessions cost more","Short sessions limit compromise but degrade faster during an outage","There is none","Long sessions are always safer"],"answer":1,"why":"Name the trade-off rather than pretending it does not exist; it is a deliberate choice with two legitimate answers.","whyWrong":["Cost is not the factor.","","The two goals genuinely conflict.","Long sessions widen the compromise window."]}],"run4":[{"q":"Which identity metric should you instrument first?","options":["IdP CPU usage","Time to deprovision, leaver event to access actually gone","Number of groups","Token size"],"answer":1,"why":"It is the number audits actually fail on, and it is usually far worse than anyone assumes. Measure it before claiming it.","whyWrong":["Infrastructure health says nothing about identity outcomes.","","A count, not an outcome.","Irrelevant to governance."]},{"q":"Which single log field most improves your ability to investigate a login failure?","options":["The response size","The server hostname","The user agent","A correlation id spanning the redirect chain"],"answer":3,"why":"An OIDC login crosses the app, the IdP and back. Without a shared id you cannot follow one user through three systems.","whyWrong":["Not useful here.","Useful, but not the linking field.","Occasionally useful, rarely decisive.",""]},{"q":"A middleware dumps request headers on error. Why is that dangerous?","options":["Headers are encrypted at the transport layer, so what gets logged is unreadable anyway","Bearer tokens accumulate in a log platform that usually has broader access than production","It breaks tracing, because the dumped headers duplicate the span context the collector expects","It is slow, since serializing every header on the error path adds latency exactly when load is high"],"answer":1,"why":"Redact at the logging layer rather than each call site, so it cannot be forgotten.","whyWrong":["Headers are plaintext at the application.","","Tracing still works.","Performance is not the issue."]},{"q":"Why is \"login failed\" an inadequate log line?","options":["It should be a warning","It lacks a timestamp","It omits the reason, which is the field that makes the failure reconstructable","It is too short"],"answer":2,"why":"Origin mismatch, expired token and unknown user are entirely different incidents that produce the same generic message.","whyWrong":["Severity is a detail.","Timestamps are usually present.","","Length is not the problem."]},{"q":"Why is identity log retention a security decision?","options":["These logs are often the shortest-retained and the first needed, so a short window prevents establishing when an attacker got in","Storage is the binding constraint, so retention is chosen by whatever the logging budget will comfortably absorb","Regulation fixes the window at thirty days, so the decision is a compliance question rather than a security one","Writing identity events synchronously slows the IdP, so shorter retention keeps authentication latency down"],"answer":0,"why":"An investigation that can only see seven days cannot answer the question that matters.","whyWrong":["","Cost is a constraint, not the reason.","Requirements vary and are rarely the binding factor.","Retention does not affect performance."]}],"run5":[{"q":"Where do most valuable identity tests belong?","options":["End to end against a real IdP","Manual testing","Load tests","Unit level, token validation and authorization logic need no network"],"answer":3,"why":"E2E tests are slow and flaky and prove only that the wiring is connected; the bugs that matter are in validation logic a unit test catches in milliseconds.","whyWrong":["Useful for wiring, and a poor place for logic.","Not repeatable.","A different concern.",""]},{"q":"Which test case do teams almost never write?","options":["An expired token","A wrong password","The happy path","A record belonging to another tenant, the IDOR test"],"answer":3,"why":"Broken object-level authorization tops the OWASP API risks and produces no error when it is missing.","whyWrong":["Commonly covered.","Commonly covered.","Everyone writes that one.",""]},{"q":"Why sign test tokens with your own generated key pair?","options":["It avoids licensing cost, since test tokens issued by the real provider count against the seat quota","It lets you produce expired, malformed and wrongly-audienced tokens on demand and deterministically","It is faster, since a local key avoids a network round trip on every token the test suite mints","Real identity providers forbid automated testing, so a local key is the only permitted route"],"answer":1,"why":"A real IdP will not issue you a broken token, which is exactly what the negative tests need.","whyWrong":["Not the reason.","","Speed is a side benefit.","They do not."]},{"q":"What is wrong with an \"auth off in dev\" switch?","options":["It has a habit of shipping, and it means the path you tested is not the path you run","It breaks the build","It is hard to implement","Nothing, it speeds development"],"answer":0,"why":"Use a mock IdP instead, so the real code path is exercised everywhere.","whyWrong":["","It does not.","It is trivially easy, which is the problem.","The convenience is real and the risk outweighs it."]}],"run6":[{"q":"What is the first thing to establish when diagnosing a login failure?","options":["The user password age","The server load","The last step of the flow that worked","Which browser the user has"],"answer":2,"why":"The failure is almost never where the error appears. Locating it in the chain eliminates most hypotheses at once.","whyWrong":["Almost never relevant.","Unrelated.","","Occasionally relevant, rarely the cause."]},{"q":"A user reports a 403 immediately after logging in successfully. Where do you look?","options":["The IdP configuration","The password policy","The MFA enrollment","Authorization, they authenticated fine and then could not see something"],"answer":3,"why":"The most commonly misdiagnosed identity symptom. Authentication and authorization have different owners and different fixes.","whyWrong":["Authentication demonstrably worked.","Not involved after a successful login.","Not involved after a successful login.",""]},{"q":"The app and IdP redirect back and forth in a loop. What is the usual cause?","options":["A wrong client secret, so the token exchange fails and the app restarts the flow each time","The app cannot set or read its cookie: SameSite, Secure over http, or a domain mismatch","A slow network, where the redirect times out before the session cookie has been written","An expired token, which the app keeps presenting and the provider keeps refusing to accept"],"answer":1,"why":"The app never sees its own session, so it starts the flow again, forever.","whyWrong":["That fails at the token endpoint instead.","","Latency does not loop.","Expiry produces an error, not a loop."]},{"q":"Nothing changed and it broke anyway. What class of bug is this?","options":["A memory leak, which had been accumulating quietly until the process finally crossed its limit","A DNS failure, where a record that had resolved for months stopped resolving without warning","A race condition, where two code paths that always overlapped happened to interleave differently","An expiry, a certificate, client secret, metadata signing cert or CRL reached its date"],"answer":3,"why":"These fail suddenly and completely at whatever hour they were issued years earlier. Alert on expiry dates well in advance.","whyWrong":["Leaks degrade gradually.","Possible, but expiry is the classic identity case.","Races are usually intermittent, not total.",""]},{"q":"Only one user is affected. What does that point to?","options":["A certificate expiry","A key rotation","Their data, account state, group membership, enrolled factors","The IdP being down"],"answer":2,"why":"Everyone affected points at configuration or keys; one user points at that user data.","whyWrong":["That would affect everyone.","That would affect everyone.","","That would affect everyone."]}],"run7":[{"q":"You are paged and it is unclear whether this is an outage or an attack. What posture do you take?","options":["Assume attack for the first ten minutes","Assume outage and restore fast","Wait for more data","Escalate and stop"],"answer":0,"why":"That stance is cheap to relax, and evidence is impossible to un-destroy.","whyWrong":["","Restoring carelessly can destroy evidence or hand access straight back.","Waiting is itself a decision, and a poor one.","Escalation does not replace containment."]},{"q":"Which signal most suggests an attack rather than an outage?","options":["A raised error rate","A uniform failure across all users","New clients, keys, factors or federated trusts appearing","Errors starting at a deploy"],"answer":2,"why":"Outages do not create artifacts. Successes where there should be failures are the other tell.","whyWrong":["A normal error rate with unusual activity is the worrying case.","Uniformity suggests an outage.","","A deploy correlation suggests an outage."]},{"q":"Why is a rolling restart risky during a suspected attack?","options":["It invalidates issued certificates, so every client has to re-establish trust before it can connect","It clears in-memory session state and the attacker foothold together with your ability to see what they did","It triggers alerts across monitoring, burying the signals that the responders actually need to see","It takes too long, and the window while nodes cycle is exactly when the service is least available"],"answer":1,"why":"Contain and preserve evidence before restoring.","whyWrong":["Restarts do not affect certificates.","","Alerts are wanted.","Duration is not the concern."]},{"q":"What single communication most reduces the load on a responder?","options":["A status page color change, which signals severity without anyone having to write prose","A detailed root-cause analysis posted early, so nobody has to ask what actually went wrong","Saying what is broken AND what still works, such as \"existing sessions are unaffected\"","A clear apology to affected customers, which cuts the volume of inbound complaints"],"answer":2,"why":"It is genuinely useful to thousands of people and stops the support flood that otherwise consumes the responder.","whyWrong":["Too little information.","Far too early, and not what people need.","","Sentiment does not inform anyone."]},{"q":"An incident is resolved. What makes the review worthwhile?","options":["Identifying who caused it","Concrete changes, an earlier alert, a capped cache TTL, a runbook not behind SSO","A written apology","Adding a manual check"],"answer":1,"why":"An incident that produces no change to the system will happen again. \"Be more careful\" is not an action.","whyWrong":["Blameless reviews find causes, not culprits.","","Does not prevent recurrence.","Manual checks decay."]}],"run8":[{"q":"Which identity change cannot be undone by reverting configuration?","options":["Changing the RP ID, every passkey registered under it is permanently unusable","A key rotation","Enabling MFA enforcement","Adding a new client"],"answer":0,"why":"Ask of any change: what will exist afterwards that did not before, and what happens to it if I revert?","whyWrong":["","Publishing new alongside old leaves a gap to revert into.","Enrollments already completed are harmless.","New clients can simply be removed."]},{"q":"What makes a rollout revertible?","options":["A feature flag, so the new path can be switched off without redeploying the previous build","A canary, so a small share of traffic meets the change before the rest of the fleet does","Accepting the new value alongside the old, and removing the old only once the new is proven","A maintenance window, so any rollback happens while nobody is depending on the service"],"answer":2,"why":"Two phases with a gap. Removing the old thing immediately is the common mistake that makes revert impossible.","whyWrong":["Useful, but the additive phase is what creates the escape route.","Canaries limit exposure without creating a revert path.","","A window does not make a change reversible."]},{"q":"Why are authorization changes more dangerous than authentication changes?","options":["They are harder to write","They need more testing time","They affect more systems","An over-permissive policy fails silently, no errors, no alerts, no complaints"],"answer":3,"why":"A policy that denies too much generates tickets within the hour; one that permits too much generates nothing at all.","whyWrong":["Difficulty is not the issue.","Time is not the distinction.","Not necessarily true.",""]},{"q":"How should you validate an authorization change before rollout?","options":["Diff the effective permissions before and after, and test the negative cases explicitly","Ask the policy author what they intended, since they know which cases the change is meant to cover","Deploy to staging and watch for errors, since real traffic exposes what review would miss","Review the policy text carefully, since the wording is what will be evaluated at runtime"],"answer":0,"why":"Policy text can look correct and compose into something far broader once other rules apply.","whyWrong":["","Author review is not verification.","Staging rarely has realistic permission data.","Text review misses interactions."]},{"q":"If the IdP is unavailable for four hours, what still works?","options":["Only read operations","Only admin tooling","Nothing","Existing sessions, if their lifetime exceeds the outage"],"answer":3,"why":"That is the whole answer, which makes session lifetime a continuity parameter as much as a security one.","whyWrong":["The distinction is session age, not operation type.","Admin tooling is usually behind the IdP too.","Established sessions are unaffected.",""]}],"run9":[{"q":"Why do feature comparisons rarely discriminate between identity vendors?","options":["Buyers lack expertise","Vendors hide features","Features change too fast","Every serious product does OIDC, SAML, SCIM and MFA, so everyone scores well"],"answer":3,"why":"Constraints discriminate. Start from your own estate, not from the market.","whyWrong":["Even experts get nothing from a feature grid.","Feature lists are published.","Churn is not the reason.",""]},{"q":"Which single question eliminates most of the market immediately?","options":["Which cloud does it run on","Who are the users, workforce, customers, partners or machines","How many integrations exist","What does it cost"],"answer":1,"why":"Workforce IAM optimises for governance and lifecycle; CIAM for conversion, scale and privacy. Using one for the other is a common and expensive mistake.","whyWrong":["Rarely decisive.","","Integration counts are marketing.","Important, and it does not narrow the field."]},{"q":"Which part of your application estate determines the project?","options":["The largest application","The newest applications","The modern OIDC majority","The awkward tail, header-based auth, Kerberos, direct LDAP binds, hardcoded passwords"],"answer":3,"why":"Ask every vendor how they handle your three worst applications, by name.","whyWrong":["Size is not difficulty.","New apps are the easy case.","Those integrate easily and consume little of the timeline.",""]},{"q":"Why should a gate never be folded into a weighted score?","options":["A weighted total will hide a dealbreaker behind a strong showing elsewhere","Scores are more accurate","It complicates the maths","Gates are subjective"],"answer":0,"why":"Fail a gate and the option is out, with no score at all, that is what makes the evaluation honest.","whyWrong":["","A score is not more accurate than a constraint.","Arithmetic is not the concern.","Gates should be the most objective criteria."]},{"q":"What is usually the largest and most underestimated cost of an identity decision?","options":["Training","Hardware","Migration and ongoing operation","The licence"],"answer":2,"why":"Weight migration effort and operational burden most heavily; licence price and developer experience dominate most evaluations for no good reason.","whyWrong":["A minor line item.","Usually irrelevant now.","","The most visible cost, rarely the largest."]},{"q":"What should a recommendation to a decision-maker include?","options":["A single winner and nothing else, so the decision-maker is not slowed down by alternatives they did not ask for","Only the technical analysis, leaving the choice itself to the person with the authority to make it","Two or three viable options with trade-offs, total cost, risks, reversibility, and what happens if you do nothing","The vendor proposals in full, so the decision-maker can weigh the original material rather than your summary"],"answer":2,"why":"Decision-makers want a defensible choice they can own. \"What happens if we do nothing\" is often the strongest argument and the most commonly omitted.","whyWrong":["That asks them to rubber-stamp rather than decide.","Cost and risk are the parts they own.","","Raw proposals are not analysis."]}],"ei1b":[{"q":"What is the security boundary in Active Directory?","options":["The forest","The site","The domain","The organizational unit"],"answer":0,"why":"Compromise anywhere in a forest is compromise everywhere. Splitting into domains gives administrative separation and almost no security separation.","whyWrong":["","A network topology concept.","A replication and administration boundary, however much the org chart wishes otherwise.","Where policy is applied and administration delegated."]},{"q":"Domain A trusts domain B. Who can access whose resources?","options":["B users can access A resources","A users can access B resources","Both directions","Neither until configured further"],"answer":0,"why":"Trust direction is the opposite of access direction, which is why people routinely configure it backwards.","whyWrong":["","That is the intuition, and it is inverted.","Only with a two-way trust.","The trust itself enables it."]},{"q":"What does DCSync abuse consist of?","options":["Stealing a backup tape containing the directory database and extracting the hashes from it","Brute-forcing the KDC until a service ticket is issued for an account the attacker does not hold","Using replication rights to ask a DC for every password hash, a legitimate protocol operation","Malware installed on a domain controller, which then exfiltrates the directory database offline"],"answer":2,"why":"No malware and no access to the DC is required, which is why replication-rights grants deserve their own alert.","whyWrong":["That is NTDS.dit theft, a different path.","Nothing is brute-forced.","","No code needs to run on the DC."]},{"q":"Why is a Domain Admin logging into a workstation dangerous?","options":["Workstations lack auditing","It violates licensing","It is slow","Their credential material then exists on the lowest-tier machine in the estate"],"answer":3,"why":"Credentials never flow downward. This single discipline breaks most real attack paths.","whyWrong":["Auditing exists on workstations.","Not a licensing matter.","Performance is irrelevant.",""]},{"q":"Why do tools that map AD attack paths exist?","options":["Active Directory has no real permission model, so tools have to infer one from observed behaviour","Microsoft requires such tooling for compliance attestation, so every audited environment runs one","Directory permissions are undocumented by Microsoft, so the only way to learn them is to probe a live domain","No single permission looks alarming, but a chain of them reaches Domain Admin, auditing one at a time never finds it"],"answer":3,"why":"AD is a graph and attackers think in paths. Defenders should run the same analysis first.","whyWrong":["It has a very detailed one.","They are third-party tools.","Permissions are visible; the problem is composition.",""]},{"q":"In a hybrid deployment, what tier is the directory-sync server?","options":["Tier 0, it holds credential material for the whole directory and can write to both sides","Tier 2, it is an application server that happens to move records between two directories","Tier 1, since it manages servers and workstations but never the directory itself","It depends on the sync model, and password hash sync makes it a tier 2 system"],"answer":0,"why":"It is frequently treated as an ordinary server, which is one of the most common serious misconfigurations in hybrid estates.","whyWrong":["","That is the mistake.","Still too low.","Every model gives it enormous reach."]}],"ei2b":[{"q":"A Kerberos service ticket is encrypted with which key?","options":["The krbtgt key","The KDC public key","The service account key","The user key"],"answer":2,"why":"That single fact is the whole of Kerberoasting: request tickets for every SPN and crack them offline with no failed logins.","whyWrong":["That encrypts the TGT.","Kerberos is symmetric here.","","The user key protects pre-authentication."]},{"q":"Why can any authenticated user request a service ticket for any SPN?","options":["It is how the protocol distributes tickets, authorization happens at the service, not the KDC","Only administrators can, since requesting a ticket for a service requires elevated privilege","It requires group membership, so a user outside the service's group is refused by the KDC","It is a misconfiguration in most domains, and correcting it removes the exposure entirely"],"answer":0,"why":"Not a bug, but it means service account password strength is the real control. gMSA with machine-generated passwords removes the problem.","whyWrong":["","Any authenticated principal can.","No membership is required.","It is default protocol behaviour."]},{"q":"What does stealing the krbtgt key allow?","options":["Resetting passwords","Reading all mail","Forging a TGT for any user with any group SIDs in the PAC","Decrypting TLS"],"answer":2,"why":"The golden ticket. This is why krbtgt compromise means rebuilding or rotating, not resetting admin passwords.","whyWrong":["Forgery is more powerful than a reset.","Not directly.","","Unrelated to TLS."]},{"q":"Why must the krbtgt key be rotated twice?","options":["The KDC accepts the previous key, so one rotation leaves forged tickets working","To update all domain controllers","For redundancy","It is a Microsoft bug"],"answer":0,"why":"One rotation feels like remediation and achieves nothing.","whyWrong":["","Replication is separate.","Not a redundancy measure.","Intentional behaviour for continuity."]},{"q":"What makes a silver ticket stealthier than a golden ticket?","options":["It is smaller","It uses stronger encryption","The KDC is never contacted, so there is no ticket request to log","It never expires"],"answer":2,"why":"A service validates a ticket entirely offline with its own key, so forging one leaves no trace at the KDC.","whyWrong":["Size is irrelevant.","Encryption is the same.","","It expires normally."]},{"q":"Why is unconstrained delegation so dangerous?","options":["It bypasses multi-factor authentication, since the forwarded ticket carries no record of how it was obtained","It is slow, because every request forces an additional round trip to the domain controller","It relies on RC4 for ticket encryption, so the material can be cracked offline given enough samples","The user entire TGT is cached on the service, so any admin there can become that user anywhere"],"answer":3,"why":"Worse still, coercing a domain controller to authenticate to such a host yields the DC TGT. Treat any such host as Tier 0.","whyWrong":["MFA is not part of the Kerberos exchange.","Performance is not the issue.","Encryption type is a separate concern.",""]},{"q":"Why is resource-based constrained delegation a common escalation path?","options":["It has no time limit, so a delegation configured once remains usable indefinitely without renewal","The attribute lives on the target computer object, so anyone who can write to it can grant themselves delegation","It is enabled by default on every domain controller, so most environments are exposed without configuring anything","It leaves no trace in the security event log, so defenders have no way to detect that it was used"],"answer":1,"why":"A permissions problem rather than a protocol flaw, which is why write access to computer objects deserves auditing.","whyWrong":["Tickets still expire.","","It must be configured.","It is auditable."]}],"s2s9":[{"q":"What assumption do AI agents break that every protocol so far relied on?","options":["That tokens expire","That TLS is available","That scopes exist","That a human is present at the moment of authorization"],"answer":3,"why":"Consent screens, step-up prompts and re-authentication all assume someone is there to respond. An agent working at 2am is not.","whyWrong":["Expiry still works normally.","Transport is unchanged.","Scopes still apply, just too coarsely.",""]},{"q":"How many identities should an agent call carry?","options":["One, the agent","Two, the user as subject, and the agent as the acting party","Three, including the tool","One (the user)"],"answer":1,"why":"Presenting only the user identity is impersonation, and nothing downstream can tell that software rather than a person decided.","whyWrong":["That loses whose data is at stake.","","The tool is the audience, not an identity in the chain.","That loses the fact that an agent acted."]},{"q":"A sub-agent requests a scope its parent was never granted. What should happen?","options":["Allow, sub-agents are trusted","Escalate to the user","Deny, narrowing must be monotonic","Allow it if the user consented once"],"answer":2,"why":"If any hop can request more than it was given, the whole chain is only as strong as its most compromised link.","whyWrong":["Trust does not travel upward in privilege.","The correct answer is to refuse, not to ask.","","Consent was for the original grant, not an expansion."]},{"q":"Why is prompt injection an authorization problem rather than a model problem?","options":["Models cannot be improved past a point, so the residual failure rate has to be handled elsewhere","Hostile content can only cause actions the agent token already permits, so the fix is not granting the authority","It is a network issue, since the injected content arrives over a channel nobody is inspecting","Prompts are unauthenticated, so any caller can submit one and the model has no way to verify the sender"],"answer":1,"why":"The token is the control, not the prompt. This is the most important consequence of agents for identity work.","whyWrong":["Models improve, and it does not close this.","","Nothing to do with the network.","True but not the mechanism that limits damage."]},{"q":"Which action should return to a human even when the agent is authorized?","options":["Listing records","Sending money","Searching an index","Reading a document"],"answer":1,"why":"Reads and reversible writes proceed; irreversible or externally-visible actions escalate. Design the interrupt before it is needed.","whyWrong":["Reversible and low risk.","","Reversible and low risk.","Reversible and low risk."]}],"az9":[{"q":"In Zanzibar, how are permissions stored?","options":["As a permission list per user","They are not stored, they are derived by walking relation tuples","As roles","As an access control matrix"],"answer":1,"why":"Everything is a subject-relation-object tuple, and a check is a graph traversal. That is what makes the model expressive and every check a traversal.","whyWrong":["No such list exists.","","Roles are one thing you can model with tuples.","A matrix would not express nesting."]},{"q":"What is the \"new enemy\" problem?","options":["A newly emerged class of attacker that existing authorization models were never designed to handle","A denial-of-service attack in which repeated permission checks exhaust the authorization service","A caching bug in clients, where a stale decision is reused after the underlying policy has changed","A stale replica applies a later write without an earlier one, so a removed user sees newly added content"],"answer":3,"why":"Each write was correct; the order was lost. This is why eventual consistency is unacceptable for authorization.","whyWrong":["Not about attacker taxonomy.","Availability is a separate concern.","It is a distributed-systems ordering problem, not a client bug.",""]},{"q":"What does a zookie do?","options":["Tells the system to evaluate against a snapshot at least as recent as an observed write","Identifies the user, carrying the subject identifier the authorization service evaluates against","Signs the request, so the authorization service can confirm which caller asked the question","Caches the decision, so a repeated check for the same subject and object avoids a round trip"],"answer":0,"why":"The client carries a token forward rather than needing a global clock, and the system guarantees it will not answer from an older state.","whyWrong":["","It carries no identity.","It is not a signature.","It constrains freshness rather than caching."]},{"q":"What does a central authorization service cost you?","options":["Compatibility with OAuth","Nothing, it is purely a win","Storage only","A hard runtime dependency on the critical path of every request"],"answer":3,"why":"Weigh it honestly, and decide deliberately what happens when the service is unreachable, fail closed and an authorization outage is a total outage.","whyWrong":["It composes fine with OAuth.","There is a real trade.","Storage is the minor cost.",""]},{"q":"When do you probably NOT need a relationship graph?","options":["When users share arbitrarily with each other","When you have nested folders","When many services must agree","When roles map cleanly to permissions and authorization is per-endpoint"],"answer":3,"why":"Most applications do not need Zanzibar; a tenant-scoped query with an ownership check is the right answer.","whyWrong":["That is exactly when you do need it.","Nesting is the case it exists for.","Shared answers are a reason to centralise.",""]}],"oa12":[{"q":"What problem does OpenID Federation solve?","options":["Slow logins","Token expiry","Weak signatures","Bilateral trust scaling quadratically, everyone configuring everyone"],"answer":3,"why":"Ten parties need forty-five relationships. Ecosystems with thousands of participants need trust proven on demand instead.","whyWrong":["It adds resolution work rather than removing it.","Unrelated to expiry.","Signatures are unchanged.",""]},{"q":"How does a relying party trust an OP it has never been configured with?","options":["By walking a signed chain of entity statements to a trust anchor it holds","By checking DNS","Through a shared secret","It trusts all OPs"],"answer":0,"why":"Trust is transitive through the anchor rather than pairwise, which is what makes onboarding a registration rather than N integrations.","whyWrong":["","DNS proves nothing about policy.","No secret is shared.","That would be no trust model at all."]},{"q":"An entity declares client_secret_basic but the anchor policy allows only private_key_jwt and mTLS. What happens?","options":["It falls back to a default","It is accepted, entities declare their own metadata","The anchor is updated","It is rejected, policies compose downward and can only narrow"],"answer":3,"why":"This is how an ecosystem enforces a security baseline on participants it does not operate, without relying on their honesty.","whyWrong":["There is no widening fallback.","Self-declaration is exactly what policy constrains.","Members do not amend the anchor.",""]},{"q":"What is the greatest risk in a federation of this kind?","options":["Chain length","Metadata size","Slow DNS","Trust anchor compromise, it takes the entire ecosystem with it"],"answer":3,"why":"Anchor keys belong offline with a rehearsed rotation. It is the trust-anchor lesson at maximum stakes.","whyWrong":["Length affects performance, not integrity.","Size is an engineering detail.","Not a trust concern.",""]},{"q":"When is bilateral registration still the better choice?","options":["For a handful of integrations that rarely change","Always","For national ecosystems","For wallet verifiers"],"answer":0,"why":"The crossover is somewhere in the tens of participants, or wherever participants change often.","whyWrong":["","Not at ecosystem scale.","Those are the federation case.","Verifiers must accept issuers they never contacted."]}],"ao8":[{"q":"What does CAE change about token handling?","options":["Tokens are encrypted, so only the intended resource server can read the claims they contain","The issuer pushes an event when something changes, so long-lived tokens can be rejected immediately","Tokens are validated by the issuer on every call","Tokens become opaque, so a resource server can no longer read claims without introspecting"],"answer":1,"why":"The token is unchanged and still cryptographically valid; the verifier now holds a fact that overrides it.","whyWrong":["Encryption is unrelated.","","That is introspection, which reintroduces the round trip.","The format is unchanged."]},{"q":"What is a Security Event Token?","options":["A refresh token","An access token with extra scopes","An encrypted session cookie","A JWT whose payload is an event rather than an identity"],"answer":3,"why":"It must be verified exactly like any other token, an unauthenticated revocation endpoint is a denial-of-service tool.","whyWrong":["Not a credential at all.","It grants no access.","Not a cookie.",""]},{"q":"What is the real cost of participating in CAE?","options":["Licence fees, since participating requires a premium tier from the identity provider","Larger tokens, since each one has to carry the claims needed to evaluate a continuous policy","Slower signatures, since every token must be re-signed whenever the evaluated context changes","The resource server becomes stateful, it must keep a revocation list and consult it"],"answer":3,"why":"That is precisely the property self-contained tokens were adopted to avoid, so it is a considered trade rather than a free win.","whyWrong":["Not a commercial matter.","Token size is unchanged.","Verification cost is unchanged.",""]},{"q":"Push delivery of an event fails and nobody notices. What is the danger?","options":["Duplicate events","The system degrades silently into no revocation, which looks fine","Tokens expire early","Events arrive out of order"],"answer":1,"why":"The worst failure mode is the one that looks healthy. Periodic reconciliation, or a short enough lifetime, is still required.","whyWrong":["Handled by jti replay checks.","","Expiry is unaffected.","A real concern, but silent loss is worse."]},{"q":"Does CAE make token revocation instant?","options":["No, it narrows the window from minutes to seconds, but a token already in flight is still honoured","Only for refresh tokens, since access tokens remain valid until their own expiry regardless","Yes, completely, since the event reaches every relying party before the next request is served","Yes, provided tokens are opaque, since the resource server must introspect each one anyway"],"answer":0,"why":"No mechanism recalls a token already in flight. Layer it: short lifetimes as the floor, CAE where seconds matter, grant revocation to stop continued access.","whyWrong":["","It applies to access tokens too.","Nothing is instant across a network.","Opaque tokens use introspection instead."]}],"iddid2":[{"q":"In the wallet model, who does the verifier contact?","options":["The wallet vendor, which vouches that the holder's device has not been tampered with","A central registry, which records every presentation so that issuers can audit their credentials","The issuer, to confirm the credential is still valid before accepting what the holder presented","Nobody, the credential is verified from its signature, so the issuer never learns where it was used"],"answer":3,"why":"That absence is the substantive difference from federation, where the IdP is online at the moment of use and therefore sees every login.","whyWrong":["The vendor is not in the loop.","Registries help find issuers, not to verify each use.","Contacting the issuer would recreate the surveillance surface.",""]},{"q":"A bar needs to check you are over 18. What should it ask for?","options":["An age_over_18 predicate claim","Your date of birth","Your full credential","Your name and address"],"answer":0,"why":"Ask for the predicate, not the data. Most systems that store dates of birth only ever needed a boolean.","whyWrong":["","More than the question requires.","Maximal over-disclosure.","Entirely unnecessary."]},{"q":"What is the mDL, and why does it differ from OID4VP?","options":["A credential format used only within the EU, which OID4VP supersedes elsewhere in the world","An older revision of OID4VP that remains in place until deployments finish migrating","A competing wallet application that implements the same protocol under a different brand","An ISO standard for mobile driving licences, using CBOR and designed to work offline over NFC or Bluetooth"],"answer":3,"why":"It came from standards bodies serving physical documents rather than from the web. Both are deployed, and convergence is partial.","whyWrong":["Adoption is wider than one region.","They developed in parallel, not in sequence.","It is a standard, not an app.",""]},{"q":"Why is credential revocation awkward in this model?","options":["Wallets cannot be updated","Issuers refuse to support it","Checking a status list can reintroduce a call that reveals you are being verified","Credentials never expire"],"answer":2,"why":"The privacy problem returns through the back door, which is why revocation remains one of the unsettled areas.","whyWrong":["Wallets update normally.","Issuers do support status mechanisms.","","They do expire."]},{"q":"What does verifiable presentation NOT solve about age assurance?","options":["Disclosing a birthdate","Proving the claim cryptographically","Working offline","That somebody must still have verified the underlying fact against a real document"],"answer":3,"why":"The privacy question moves rather than disappearing: from what the site learns to who issued this and what they retained.","whyWrong":["Selective disclosure solves exactly that.","The cryptography works.","mDL works offline by design.",""]}],"ig7":[{"q":"Roughly how do non-human identities compare in number to human ones?","options":["Far fewer","About equal","It varies with no pattern","They outnumber humans substantially, commonly ten to one or more"],"answer":3,"why":"And almost every governance control in the domain was designed for the humans, which is why NHIs are more numerous, more privileged and less governed.","whyWrong":["The opposite is true.","Not close to equal in most estates.","The pattern is consistent.",""]},{"q":"Why does joiner-mover-leaver not work for service accounts?","options":["Nothing signals that a service account is finished, so nothing ever removes it","They have no manager","They cannot be disabled","HR does not track them"],"answer":0,"why":"Estates accumulate them monotonically. An expiry that must be renewed converts \"forever\" into a deliberate decision.","whyWrong":["","True, and the ownership gap is downstream of the lifecycle gap.","They can be disabled; nobody dares.","A symptom rather than the mechanism."]},{"q":"What is the single most common finding about non-human identities?","options":["Wrong naming","No current owner, nobody knows what it does, so nobody will disable it","Missing tags","Weak passwords"],"answer":1,"why":"An identity without a named, current owner is ungovernable by definition.","whyWrong":["Cosmetic, and not what makes it ungovernable.","","Cosmetic, and not what makes it ungovernable.","Common, but downstream of ownership."]},{"q":"What is the strongest control for a workload credential?","options":["Storing it encrypted","A longer secret","Workload identity federation, no static credential exists at all","Quarterly rotation"],"answer":2,"why":"There is then nothing to leak, rotate, or find in a repository.","whyWrong":["It must be decrypted to be used.","Length does not prevent leakage.","","Better than nothing, and still a secret."]},{"q":"Why do reviewers rubber-stamp non-human access reviews?","options":["Nobody recognises svc-etl-prod-3, so without evidence of what it does they cannot make a real decision","The tooling is slow, so reviewers batch-approve to get through the queue before it times out","They are lazy, and the review is one more task competing with work they consider more valuable","Reviews come round too frequently, so the same entries are approved repeatedly without fresh thought"],"answer":0,"why":"The fix is better evidence, last run, what it accessed, who owns it, not more reviews. Asking anyway trains people that reviews are theatre.","whyWrong":["","Tooling speed is not the barrier.","They are given an impossible task.","Frequency is not the barrier."]}]};
